const BQ=[
{"id":"P1","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A telecom operator wants to automate change-of-address requests. Customers send free-form emails and scanned forms, the new address must be validated against the national postal database, and the customer record must be updated in the CRM. Errors in customer records cause missed bills and complaints. Which TWO elements belong in the design? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude handles each request from email to CRM update, including the final approval, so that the process stays in one model call with fewer handoffs."},{"l":"B","t":"Claude extracts the fields, checks the address against its own knowledge of postal codes, and writes the result straight into the CRM."},{"l":"C","t":"Low-confidence extractions go to a person for review."},{"l":"D","t":"A human agent retypes every request into the CRM, and Claude only drafts the confirmation email, so that no automated step touches customer records."},{"l":"E","t":"Claude extracts the fields; the postal database validates them; the CRM's API writes the update."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Decompose into three owners. Claude does the language work (extraction), existing systems own what is already reliable (postal validation, CRM writes), and people own exceptions. Letting Claude validate from memory or approve its own work over-assigns it; the manual option under-uses it.","x":["Incorrect. One call that approves its own output removes the checks that matter.","Incorrect. A model's memory of postal codes is not a source of truth.","Correct. People handle the cases Claude is unsure of.","Incorrect. Retyping everything wastes Claude's strength at reading free-form input.","Correct. Each step goes to the owner that does it reliably."],"df":"easy","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P3","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A platform team wants Claude to help diagnose intermittent production incidents. Each incident involves different services, logs, and metrics, so the investigation path cannot be written in advance. The assistant would have read-only access to logs and dashboards, and engineers act on its findings. Which design is most appropriate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A fixed workflow that runs the same predefined sequence of log queries for every incident, keeping behaviour predictable."},{"l":"B","t":"An agent with a constrained read-only tool set, a per-run turn and token budget, and explicit stopping criteria."},{"l":"C","t":"A single augmented call that receives a full day of raw logs in the prompt and returns a diagnosis in one pass."},{"l":"D","t":"A RAG pipeline over last year's postmortems that answers the incident question directly from similar past cases."}],"a":["B"],"r":"When the path cannot be enumerated and the cost of a wrong intermediate step is recoverable (read-only, human acts on the output), an agent is justified, but only with constrained tools, budgets, and stopping criteria. A fixed workflow cannot adapt, a single call cannot fit or navigate the logs, and past postmortems are a snapshot, not live state.","x":["Incorrect. A fixed sequence of queries cannot adapt when each incident involves different services, logs and metrics. The investigation path is unknown in advance, which is the case for an agent.","Correct. When the path cannot be written in advance and the tools are read-only with a human acting on the findings, an agent is justified. The tool limits, turn and token budget, and stopping criteria keep it bounded.","Incorrect. A full day of raw logs will not fit or be navigable in one prompt, and one pass cannot follow leads from one service to the next.","Incorrect. Past postmortems are a snapshot of old incidents, not live state. They can inform the investigation but cannot answer the current incident on their own."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P7","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"match","q":"A market-research firm runs a multi-agent system overnight: an orchestrator splits each study into sub-questions for eight subagents and combines their findings. The architect reviews six failures from the past quarter. Match each failure to the design response that addresses it. A response can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Checkpoint the orchestrator's progress so that a run can resume"},{"l":"B","t":"Validate the subagent's output, then retry or re-route it"},{"l":"C","t":"Propagate a shared trace ID across all agents"},{"l":"D","t":"Apply an agreed conflict rule and show the disagreement"}],"st":["The orchestrator crashes and loses its goal and synthesis state.","A subagent returns malformed output.","Nobody can tell which subagent calls belonged to which study.","Two subagents return conflicting market-size figures.","A subagent returns an empty result.","A long run fails at hour five and must not start again from zero."],"a":["A","B","C","D","B","A"],"r":"Subagent failures and conflicts are caught at recoverable boundaries, but the orchestrator holds the goal and synthesis state, so losing it is often unrecoverable. Protect it with checkpoints; validate subagent output, trace across agents, and settle conflicts by an agreed rule.","x":["Checkpoint. The orchestrator's state is the hardest thing to recover.","Validate and retry. A bad subagent result is caught at its boundary.","Trace ID. One ID ties every call to its study.","Conflict rule. A rule decides, and the disagreement is shown, not hidden.","Validate and retry. An empty result is recoverable at the subagent.","Checkpoint. A saved state lets the run resume where it stopped."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P8","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A regulated lender is deciding whether an autonomous agent should adjudicate refund-dispute cases. Every decision must be reconstructable for regulators, a wrong decision triggers audits and remediation, the deadline is hours rather than seconds, and the team can enumerate the steps for the common case. Which TWO factors most directly rule out the agent pattern? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Model size:** agents require the largest model tier, which makes them unaffordable for the lender."},{"l":"B","t":"**Latency budget:** an agent loop cannot possibly finish within a few hours, so the deadline excludes it."},{"l":"C","t":"**Observability:** the agent's path is not written in advance, while the audit requirement needs steps that log like ordinary code."},{"l":"D","t":"**Error cost:** a wrong result across several unguarded turns is expensive, whereas a workflow can place deterministic guards between steps."},{"l":"E","t":"**Context window:** an agent cannot hold a single case file, so it would lose track of the dispute details."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Apply the five factors in sequence. High error cost and a hard observability/audit requirement rule out an agent here. A multi-hour deadline is generous, agents do not inherently require the largest tier, and a case file fits comfortably in context.","x":["Incorrect. Agents do not inherently require the largest model tier, so affordability is not what rules the pattern out here.","Incorrect. A deadline of hours is generous for an agent loop, so latency does not exclude the agent.","Correct. The audit requirement needs every step reconstructable, and an agent's path is not written in advance. Steps that log like ordinary code come from a workflow.","Correct. A wrong decision triggers audits and remediation, and an agent can compound errors across unguarded turns. A workflow can place deterministic guards between steps.","Incorrect. A single case file fits comfortably in the context window, so the agent would not lose track of the dispute details."],"df":"hard","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P11","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A draft business case claims an AI contract-review assistant will cut senior-associate time by 60%. Which TWO weaknesses would a finance reviewer most likely identify? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The case states a payback period and shows how it changes if volume doubles."},{"l":"B","t":"The run cost is built from the token distribution produced by the sizing model."},{"l":"C","t":"The baseline is expressed in hours per contract, a unit the business recognises."},{"l":"D","t":"The projection treats the associate review step as eliminated although the design keeps the associate as final reviewer."},{"l":"E","t":"The baseline hours per contract were estimated from partners' intuition rather than measured."}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Two common ROI-map errors are an estimated rather than measured baseline and assuming full automation when the design requires human review (labour is reduced, not removed). Using the sizing distribution, stating payback with sensitivity, and using a recognisable business unit are all good practice.","x":["Incorrect. Stating a payback period and showing sensitivity to volume is good practice, not a weakness.","Incorrect. Building the run cost from the token distribution of the sizing model is good practice.","Incorrect. A baseline in hours per contract is a unit the business recognises, which is a strength.","Correct. The projection treats the associate review step as eliminated although the design keeps the associate as final reviewer, so labour is reduced and not removed.","Correct. A baseline estimated from partners' intuition rather than measured weakens every figure built on it."],"df":"hard","ob":"Align solutions to business value pillars (efficiency, transformation, productivity, cost, performance SLAs)"},
{"id":"P13","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An architecture sketch for a contract-drafting assistant shows requests flowing from the user into a Claude workflow and out as an approved draft delivered to the legal team. The sponsor wants the design to get better over time and wants to know when it gets worse. Which TWO elements complete the loop? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Feed those outcomes into the evaluation set and monitoring."},{"l":"B","t":"Retrain the Claude model every week on the drafts that were delivered, so that it learns the legal team's preferences."},{"l":"C","t":"Log only the token counts per request, since whether a draft was good is a question for the end user."},{"l":"D","t":"Record whether each draft was accepted, revised or rejected downstream."},{"l":"E","t":"Increase the context window, so that the model can remember its previous drafts across all later requests."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"A feedback loop needs an outcome signal. Whether the output was accepted, revised or rejected feeds evaluations and monitoring, which is how change is gated and drift is detected. Claude's weights are not retrained by your application, and token counts say nothing about quality.","x":["Correct. Outcomes become test cases and alerts.","Incorrect. The application does not retrain Claude's weights.","Incorrect. Token counts measure cost, not quality.","Correct. The downstream outcome is the quality signal.","Incorrect. A larger window does not tell the team whether drafts are good."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P18","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A 40-person claims-operations team, none of them developers, needs to ask questions over an approved set of internal policy documents and share context with each other. Nothing in the request requires retrieving live records or embedding the assistant in another product. Which recommendation fits, and on which deciding tradeoff?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"claude.ai with a Project: the deciding tradeoff is audience, and this team needs a ready-made entry point."},{"l":"B","t":"An MCP server over the document store, because the deciding tradeoff is reuse across the team's future internal tools."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code on shared laptops, because the deciding tradeoff is audience and it runs wherever the team already works."},{"l":"D","t":"The direct API behind a custom-built interface, because the deciding tradeoff is integration depth for the policy documents."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Pick the entry point by the work it has to do. With no programmatic retrieval and nothing to embed, audience is the deciding tradeoff and a ready-made product wins. Claude Code is built for engineers, a custom build adds cost the use case does not need, and MCP's reuse argument does not apply when only one client will ever consume it.","x":["Correct. With no retrieval of live records and nothing to embed, the audience is what decides. A non-developer team needs a ready-made entry point, and a Project shares context.","Incorrect. MCP's advantage is reuse across clients, and only one client would use it here, so it adds work without a benefit.","Incorrect. Claude Code is built for engineers, and this team is not made up of developers.","Incorrect. A custom interface on the API adds build cost that the use case does not need."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P111","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer's HR team wants Claude to answer employee questions about leave entitlements. The answers live in 30 policy documents that are revised every quarter, and employees must always get the current rule. Which TWO design choices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Train a custom model on the policy documents each quarter, so that the current policy is stored in the model's weights."},{"l":"B","t":"Re-index whenever a policy is revised, removing the superseded version."},{"l":"C","t":"Let Claude answer from its general knowledge of typical leave policies, with a disclaimer on every reply."},{"l":"D","t":"Retrieve from the current policy documents at question time, and cite the section used."},{"l":"E","t":"Build a fixed table of questions with canned answers, and bypass the model for anything not in the table."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Frequently changing, document-based knowledge is a retrieval problem: fetch the current text, cite it, and re-index on change. The application cannot update model weights each quarter, general knowledge is not the company's policy, and a canned table cannot cover free-form questions.","x":["Incorrect. The application does not retrain model weights, and weights would lag the policy.","Correct. Re-indexing keeps the index in step with each revision.","Incorrect. Typical policies are not this company's policy.","Correct. Current text with a citation gives a checkable answer.","Incorrect. Free-form questions do not fit a fixed table."],"df":"easy","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P112","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A manufacturer's procurement team wants to match purchase orders to goods-receipt notes and flag mismatches. The documents arrive as PDFs in many layouts, and Claude will extract the fields from both. The matching rules are exact: item code, quantity and unit price must agree within a stated tolerance. Which TWO choices complete the design? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude reads both documents and decides whether they match, since it can reason about differences in wording."},{"l":"B","t":"Code applies the matching rules and tolerances to the extracted fields."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude extracts the fields and also applies the tolerance rules, since splitting the steps adds handoffs."},{"l":"D","t":"A person keys both documents into the ERP system by hand, and Claude only summarises each mismatch."},{"l":"E","t":"Mismatches go to a person, with both documents side by side."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Claude owns the language work of reading varied documents, code owns exact rules and tolerances, and a person owns the mismatches. Asking Claude to apply exact tolerances is the over-assignment the decomposition avoids.","x":["Incorrect. A match is an exact rule, not a judgement about wording.","Correct. Code applies the tolerances the same way every time.","Incorrect. Exact tolerances belong in code.","Incorrect. Manual keying wastes the extraction Claude does well.","Correct. A person resolves mismatches with the evidence in front of them."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P113","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An electronics company's support inbox receives refund requests, technical faults and legal complaints. Each category needs a different prompt, different tools and a different escalation path, and legal complaints must never be handled by the refund flow. Which TWO design choices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give each handler its own prompt, tools and escalation path."},{"l":"B","t":"One long prompt that describes all three categories and lets the model improvise the handling for each message."},{"l":"C","t":"Classify each message first, then send it to the handler for its category."},{"l":"D","t":"An autonomous agent that decides on its own which tools to use, with no fixed categories at all."},{"l":"E","t":"Draft a reply and revise it repeatedly until a judge model is satisfied with the result."}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Distinct categories with different prompts, tools and escalation paths are the textbook case for routing. One long prompt blurs the handling, an agent adds variance the task does not need, and evaluator-optimizer addresses output quality, not input variety.","x":["Correct. Each category gets exactly the prompt, tools and escalation it needs.","Incorrect. One prompt blurs three very different flows.","Correct. Classifying first keeps legal complaints out of the refund flow.","Incorrect. An agent adds variance where fixed categories exist.","Incorrect. Revising replies does not sort messages by type."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P115","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A pharmaceutical company's research agent sometimes loops for hundreds of turns, repeating similar literature searches and burning budget without converging. Researchers still want something useful back when a question turns out to be hard. Which TWO design changes address the root cause? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Move to a larger model, on the basis that it will recognise sooner when it already has the answer and stop."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the search tool after the tenth call, so that the agent has to answer from what it already holds."},{"l":"C","t":"When the budget is reached, return a partial result with a note on what is still missing."},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the agent in its prompt to be efficient and to stop whenever it feels it has enough."},{"l":"E","t":"Set a turn and token budget, with explicit stopping criteria."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Unbounded loops are a known agent failure. The fix is structural: budgets, stopping criteria and a defined partial-result path. A larger model or a request to be efficient enforces nothing.","x":["Incorrect. A larger model can loop too; nothing stops it.","Incorrect. An arbitrary cut-off removes the tool without defining when the work is done.","Correct. A partial result still gives researchers something useful.","Incorrect. A request to be efficient is not a limit.","Correct. Budgets and stopping criteria end the loop by design."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P119","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A media company's research system runs eight subagents in parallel, and each appends its findings to one shared file. Results are occasionally lost or interleaved, and editors have found half-written paragraphs in published briefings. Which TWO design changes remove the problem? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell each subagent to wait a random number of seconds before writing, so that the writes rarely coincide."},{"l":"B","t":"Run all the subagents in one shared conversation, so that each sees the others' edits to the file as they happen."},{"l":"C","t":"Let only the orchestrator write the combined file, after merging."},{"l":"D","t":"Add a line to each subagent's prompt asking it to check the file for conflicts before writing."},{"l":"E","t":"Have each subagent return its result to the orchestrator instead of writing to the file."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Shared mutable state between parallel workers produces races. Have subagents return results to the orchestrator, which merges them in one place. Delays and polite instructions do not remove the race.","x":["Incorrect. Random delays make collisions rarer, not impossible.","Incorrect. A shared conversation mixes their contexts and still shares the file.","Correct. A single writer cannot collide with itself.","Incorrect. A check before writing still races with the next writer.","Correct. Returning results removes the shared file from the subagents' work."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P120","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"match","q":"An architect is laying out a ticket-triage system for a software company, following the ticket-triage reference architecture. Each step is given to the owner that does it best, and the outcome feeds the evaluation. Match each step to its owner. An owner can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude"},{"l":"B","t":"Deterministic code or an existing system"},{"l":"C","t":"A person"}],"st":["Classifying the ticket and extracting its fields.","Routing the ticket to a queue based on the classification.","Drafting a reply to the customer.","Reviewing low-confidence cases before they are sent.","Recording the outcome for evaluation and monitoring.","Deciding a goodwill refund above the support agent's limit."],"a":["A","B","A","C","B","C"],"r":"The reference flow is ingest, classify and extract, route, draft, human review on low confidence, and an outcome record that feeds evaluation. Language work goes to Claude, exact routing and records to code, and judgement calls to people.","x":["Claude. Classifying and extracting from free text is language work.","Code. Routing on a known classification is a rule.","Claude. Drafting a reply is language work.","Person. Uncertain cases need human judgement before sending.","Code. The outcome record is written by the system for evaluation.","Person. A refund above the limit needs someone with the authority."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P126","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A data-engineering team wants Claude to refactor pipeline code in their repositories, run the tests, and propose changes for review. Which entry point fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A claude.ai Project where engineers paste in code and copy the results back by hand."},{"l":"B","t":"A custom chat interface built on the Messages API, with no access to the repositories."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code, which works inside the repository with file, shell, and test access under permission rules."},{"l":"D","t":"An MCP server that exposes the team's documentation, with no ability to change files."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Claude Code is built for work inside a repository with file, shell, and test access under permission boundaries. Copy-paste chat, an API with no repository access, and a read-only documentation server do not do the job.","x":["Incorrect. Pasting code into a Project and copying results back is manual and gives no ability to run the tests.","Incorrect. A chat interface with no repository access cannot refactor the pipelines or run the tests.","Correct. Claude Code works inside the repository with file, shell and test access under permission rules, and proposes changes for review.","Incorrect. A read-only documentation server cannot change files, so it cannot perform the refactor."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P127","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A software vendor wants a Claude assistant inside its own product, with its own login, its own interface, and per-customer usage metering. Which entry point fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give every customer a seat on a claude.ai Team plan and ask them to use the assistant there."},{"l":"B","t":"Run Claude Code on the vendor's own servers and let each customer's users connect to it directly, since it already provides an agent loop and an interface."},{"l":"C","t":"Use an MCP server alone, because it provides the interface that customers will see."},{"l":"D","t":"Build on the API (or the Agent SDK for an agent loop) so that the vendor controls authentication, the interface, and metering."}],"a":["D"],"r":"A vendor embedding Claude in its own product needs control of authentication, interface, and metering, which the API (or Agent SDK for an agent loop) provides. claude.ai seats and Claude Code are not multi-tenant backends.","x":["Incorrect. claude.ai seats are an end-user product and do not give the vendor its own login, interface or metering.","Incorrect. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend for customers.","Incorrect. An MCP server is not an interface for customers. It exposes tools and data to a client.","Correct. Building on the API, or the Agent SDK for an agent loop, lets the vendor control authentication, the interface and metering."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P128","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An insurer's assistant emails policy explanations directly to customers with no review. It occasionally misstates the policy, and one misstatement led to a complaint to the regulator. The sponsor wants the risk reduced without losing the automation on the 90% of replies that are routine. Which TWO changes fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Send replies the check cannot confirm to a person for approval."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell the model in the prompt to be especially careful with policy statements and to double-check its work."},{"l":"C","t":"Add a disclaimer to every email saying the content was generated by AI and may contain errors."},{"l":"D","t":"Check each draft's policy statements against the policy source before sending."},{"l":"E","t":"Send every reply to a person for approval regardless of risk, so that nothing leaves unchecked."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Match control to risk: validate claims against the policy source and send only unconfirmed replies to a person. Prompting for care and disclaimers do not prevent errors, and approving everything removes the automation benefit.","x":["Correct. People see only the replies that need them.","Incorrect. Asking for care does not stop misstatements.","Incorrect. A disclaimer still sends the wrong statement.","Correct. A check against the source catches misstatements before sending.","Incorrect. Reviewing every reply throws away the routine automation."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P131","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's reporting workflow is a three-stage chain: extract figures from statements, normalise them, and draft a summary. Each stage looks acceptable when tested alone, yet about one in eight final summaries is wrong, and the team cannot say which stage is responsible. Which TWO changes should the architect make? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Replace the three stages with one much longer prompt, and drop the intermediate outputs altogether."},{"l":"B","t":"Measure each stage's output on its own against labelled examples, to find where errors start."},{"l":"C","t":"Raise the temperature on the middle stage, so that it can correct upstream mistakes through variety."},{"l":"D","t":"Run the whole chain three times and take the majority answer, without examining any intermediate output."},{"l":"E","t":"Add validation between stages, so that an error is caught where it occurs."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Chained stages compound errors, so check at the boundaries and measure each stage on its own. Merging stages or retrying blindly leaves the cause in place.","x":["Incorrect. One long prompt hides where errors happen.","Correct. Per-stage measurement shows which stage starts the errors.","Incorrect. More variation adds errors rather than fixing them.","Incorrect. A majority of wrong answers is still wrong, and the cause stays hidden.","Correct. A check between stages stops an error travelling on."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P133","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A software company's support agent sometimes cannot resolve a case after several attempts. Today it keeps trying until the customer gives up, and customers who finally reach a person must explain everything again. Which TWO changes should the architect make to the escalation design? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the customer to start a new conversation, so that the next attempt begins from a clean slate."},{"l":"B","t":"Set a clear trigger for handing off, such as a number of failed attempts."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep retrying silently with small variations until the agent produces some answer, so that no case is handed off."},{"l":"D","t":"Hand off to a person with a summary of the case, the steps tried, and the customer's goal."},{"l":"E","t":"Close the case with a standard apology message and invite the customer to try again later."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"A good escalation fires at a clear point and hands over the case summary, what was tried and the customer's goal, so that the person does not start over. Silent retries and closing the case fail the customer.","x":["Incorrect. A fresh start makes the customer repeat everything.","Correct. A trigger stops the agent looping until the customer gives up.","Incorrect. Endless retries are the problem being fixed.","Correct. A complete handoff means the customer explains nothing twice.","Incorrect. Closing the case leaves the customer without help."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P135","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An insurer's onboarding workflow timed out after calling a CRM-update tool, and the orchestrator retried the step. Afterwards the CRM held duplicate customer records, which then caused duplicate mailings. Retries are still needed, because timeouts are common at peak. Which TWO design changes prevent this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Record each side-effecting step's request key and result, so that a retry can tell what already happened."},{"l":"B","t":"Lower the timeout, so that the call fails sooner and the retry starts before the first call has finished."},{"l":"C","t":"Remove retries from the workflow, so that a failed step is never attempted twice under any circumstances."},{"l":"D","t":"Make side-effecting calls idempotent with a request key, so that a repeated call creates no second record."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the model to check whether a record exists before it calls the update tool each time."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Retries after a timeout are only safe if side effects are idempotent. A request key lets the system recognise a repeated call and avoid a second record, and recording each step's result shows what already happened.","x":["Correct. A recorded result tells the retry the record already exists.","Incorrect. A shorter timeout makes overlapping calls more likely.","Incorrect. Retries are needed at peak; the side effect is the problem.","Correct. The same key cannot create two records.","Incorrect. A model check races with the first call and is not guaranteed."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P137","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A sponsor at a large employer asks for one chatbot that handles twelve unrelated jobs, from payroll questions to IT password resets, all live within the quarter. The architect wants a design that stays safe and maintainable. Which TWO recommendations fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"One assistant with one very large prompt that holds the instructions for all twelve jobs."},{"l":"B","t":"Start with the jobs that have the most volume and clearest value, and add the rest in phases."},{"l":"C","t":"Twelve separate chatbots, each with its own login and interface, so that nothing is shared between them."},{"l":"D","t":"An autonomous agent that holds every tool in the company and works out for itself what to do."},{"l":"E","t":"A front-door router that sends each request to a focused handler with its own prompt, tools and limits."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Decompose by job: a router in front of focused handlers, each with its own prompt, tools and limits, rolled out in phases starting where the value is clearest. One giant prompt blurs the jobs, twelve silos duplicate effort, and an everything-agent maximises risk.","x":["Incorrect. Twelve jobs in one prompt interfere with each other.","Correct. Phasing delivers value early and limits risk.","Incorrect. Twelve logins and interfaces duplicate effort and confuse users.","Incorrect. Every tool in one agent is the riskiest design.","Correct. Focused handlers stay safe and maintainable."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P138","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"match","q":"An insurer is redesigning its claims process around Claude. The architect assigns each task to the owner best suited to it, keeping language work with Claude, exact work with systems, and accountable decisions with people. Match each task to its owner. An owner can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude"},{"l":"B","t":"A deterministic system"},{"l":"C","t":"A person with the authority"}],"st":["Summarising an adjuster's free-text notes into a structured case summary.","Computing the exact payout from policy terms and deductible tables.","Deciding whether a large claim is paid, and releasing the funds.","Keeping the system of record for each policy and its claim history.","Extracting claim details from photos of handwritten forms.","Approving a claim that has been flagged as possible fraud."],"a":["A","B","C","B","A","C"],"r":"Summarising and structuring free text is a core language strength. Exact payout arithmetic and the system of record belong to deterministic systems, and releasing funds or overriding a fraud flag needs a person with the authority.","x":["Claude. Summarising free text is language work.","Deterministic system. Payouts are computed exactly in code.","Person. Releasing funds is an accountable decision.","Deterministic system. The record of truth lives in an existing system.","Claude. Reading varied handwriting and photos is language and vision work.","Person. A fraud flag needs accountable judgement."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P141","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO situations favour deterministic routing in code over a Claude classifier? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The category must be inferred from the tone of a free-text message."},{"l":"B","t":"The route is fixed by a field already present on the request, such as account tier or channel."},{"l":"C","t":"The categories overlap and need subtle human-like judgement to separate."},{"l":"D","t":"The decision must be exact, repeatable, and easy to audit."},{"l":"E","t":"The incoming messages arrive in many languages and writing styles."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Use code when the route is determined by a field or when the decision must be exact and auditable. Tone, overlapping categories, and varied languages are where a model classifier adds value.","x":["Incorrect. Inferring a category from tone is what a model classifier does well, so it does not favour code.","Correct. When a field already on the request decides the route, such as account tier or channel, plain code is simpler and exact.","Incorrect. Overlapping categories that need subtle judgement are where a model classifier adds value.","Correct. Decisions that must be exact, repeatable and auditable fit deterministic code.","Incorrect. Many languages and writing styles are handled well by a model, and rules would struggle."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P20","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A lender's leadership asks the team to run every call of a new document assistant on the most capable model tier 'to be safe'. No evaluation set exists yet, and the volume will be high. Which TWO recommendations should the architect make? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Build an evaluation set with a pass mark agreed in advance, and change tiers only on its results."},{"l":"B","t":"Choose the smallest tier to guarantee low spend, and move up only if complaints begin to arrive."},{"l":"C","t":"Adopt the largest tier for every call now, since accuracy matters most, and revisit cost after the first invoice."},{"l":"D","t":"Start on the balanced tier as the default."},{"l":"E","t":"Split traffic at random across all tiers and keep whichever draws the fewest complaints over a month."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Not deciding on a tier is itself a decision, and it defaults to the most expensive option. The balanced tier is the default; moves up or down are releases gated by an evaluation with a threshold set in advance.","x":["Correct. A pass mark agreed first makes tier decisions evidence-based.","Incorrect. Waiting for complaints means users find the failures.","Incorrect. 'To be safe' is not evidence, and the cost at high volume is large.","Correct. The balanced tier is the sensible starting point.","Incorrect. Complaints are a slow, noisy signal, and random tiers confuse users."],"df":"easy","ob":"Select appropriate Claude models based on trade-offs"},
{"id":"P21","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A law firm's contract-review assistant sends a 12,000-token playbook and instruction block on every call, followed by a different clause each time. Calls arrive every few seconds, and cost and latency are both over target. Which TWO layout choices reduce them? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Summarise the playbook down to 500 tokens, so that caching becomes unnecessary and the prompt stays short."},{"l":"B","t":"Append the changing clause after the breakpoint."},{"l":"C","t":"Place the cache breakpoint after the clause, so that the entire prompt, clause included, is cached."},{"l":"D","t":"Put the clause first, so that the model reads the specific task before the long background."},{"l":"E","t":"Put the instructions and playbook first, with the cache breakpoint after them."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Caching works on a stable prefix: static content first, dynamic content after the breakpoint. Putting the changing clause first or inside the cached span defeats reuse, and summarising the playbook discards required content.","x":["Incorrect. The playbook's detail is needed for the review.","Correct. The clause changes, so it belongs after the cached prefix.","Incorrect. A prefix that includes the clause changes on every call.","Incorrect. A changing first part means nothing can be reused.","Correct. The stable 12,000 tokens are cached and reused on every call."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P23","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"yn","q":"After a release of a bank's assistant, the prompt-cache hit rate falls from about 85% to nearly zero and cost rises sharply, although traffic and the model are unchanged. The diff shows that the current date and time were added as the first line of the system prompt. For each statement, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["A cache hit needs the cached prefix to be identical on every request.","A timestamp on the first line changes the prefix on every request.","Moving the timestamp after the cache breakpoint restores the hits.","Raising `max_tokens` makes cached entries last longer.","Half of the guardrails must be deleted so that the prompt fits in the cache.","The cache also remembers facts from earlier conversations."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],"r":"A cache hit requires an identical prefix. A request-specific value at the top changes the prefix every time, so keep the fixed part genuinely fixed and put variable content after the breakpoint. Caching is not memory.","x":["Yes. Only an exact match of the prefix is reused.","Yes. The time is different on every request.","Yes. Variable content after the breakpoint leaves the prefix unchanged.","No. Output length has nothing to do with cache lifetime.","No. The length was not the problem; the changing first line was.","No. Caching reuses processing of a prefix; it stores no facts."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P24","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"yn","q":"A legal firm's summarisation service sends long case files to the API. Some requests are rejected with a 400 error before anything is generated. Others complete, but the summary stops mid-sentence and the response carries the stop reason `model_context_window_exceeded`. For each statement, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["A request rejected with a 400 error was too large before generation began.","The `model_context_window_exceeded` stop reason means the prompt fitted but generation then reached the context limit.","A summary cut off with that stop reason shows that the prompt cache expired during generation.","Counting tokens before sending, and reserving room for the output, helps with both symptoms.","Trimming or compacting the case material gives generation more room.","Both symptoms have the same cause, so one fix will remove both."],"a":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes","Yes","No"],"r":"There are two distinct edge errors. An oversized request is rejected before generation, while a request that fits but whose generation reaches the window ends with a truncated response and this stop reason. Check usage and count tokens before sending, and compact context.","x":["Yes. An oversized prompt is refused before anything is generated.","Yes. The prompt fitted, but there was not enough room left for the output.","No. Cache expiry does not cut off a response.","Yes. Counting catches oversized prompts, and reserved room prevents cut-offs.","Yes. Less input leaves more of the window for the summary.","No. One is an oversized request and the other is output running out of room."],"df":"hard","ob":"Optimize context windows and manage token usage"},
{"id":"P25","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"match","q":"A software company is choosing the prompting technique for six tasks in its support platform. It starts with the simplest technique and adds only what the task needs. Match each task to the technique that fits it best. A technique can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Zero-shot instructions"},{"l":"B","t":"Few-shot examples"},{"l":"C","t":"Step-by-step reasoning"},{"l":"D","t":"Structured outputs with a JSON schema"}],"st":["Translating a short product description into French.","Labelling ticket severity, where 'degraded but usable' and 'partially unavailable' are hard to tell apart.","Checking a refund request against several interacting policy rules, where the path matters.","Returning fields that downstream code parses.","Matching a house writing style that is easier to show than to describe.","Summarising a meeting transcript in three bullet points."],"a":["A","B","C","D","B","A"],"r":"Start zero-shot and measure. Few-shot fits judgements that are easier to show than to describe, step-by-step reasoning fits real intermediate steps, and a JSON schema fits output that code parses.","x":["Zero-shot. A clear, common task needs only instructions.","Few-shot. Examples near the boundary settle subtle categories.","Step-by-step. Interacting rules need the path worked through.","Structured outputs. A schema makes parsed output reliable.","Few-shot. Showing the style works better than describing it.","Zero-shot. A simple, well-defined task needs no examples."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P26","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"yn","q":"A retailer has moved its support assistant to a newer, more capable model. The prompt still carries a 12-example few-shot block and detailed step-by-step instructions written for the older model, and the team is unsure what to keep. For each statement, select Yes if it is sound practice or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["The same prompt can behave differently on the newer model.","A more capable model may need less scaffolding, but the evaluation should decide what goes.","All the examples should be removed at once, because newer models never need scaffolding.","Re-running the evaluation set before trimming shows what can safely be removed.","Adding more examples always helps a more capable model.","The model change should be treated as a release, gated on the evaluation."],"a":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes","No","Yes"],"r":"The same prompt does not behave identically across models. A more capable model may need less scaffolding, but that is a question the evaluation answers, not an assumption.","x":["Yes. Prompts are tuned to a model, so behaviour can change.","Yes. Less may be needed, and evidence decides how much less.","No. Removing everything untested risks regressions.","Yes. The evaluation shows which scaffolding still earns its place.","No. Extra examples add cost and can over-constrain a capable model.","Yes. A model change is a release like any other."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P29","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Five product teams need the same incident-postmortem procedure. It must be versioned, approved by compliance, and roll-back-able. Which reuse mechanism fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Embed it in each team's system prompt and let each team evolve its own copy over time."},{"l":"B","t":"Package it as a Skill: a versioned, governed unit with instructions and optional scripts."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep it in the lead engineer's personal prompt library and share it on request."},{"l":"D","t":"Share a wiki page of prompt fragments that each team copies into its own code and adapts."}],"a":["B"],"r":"When a stable procedure must be distributed across teams with versioning, approval, and rollback, a Skill fits. Copied prompts drift into many slightly different versions with quietly different behaviour.","x":["Incorrect. Separate copies in each team's system prompt drift into different versions with different behaviour, and none is versioned or approved centrally.","Correct. A Skill is a versioned, governed unit with instructions and optional scripts. It can be approved by compliance, distributed to every team and rolled back.","Incorrect. One engineer's personal library shared on request has no versioning, approval or rollback, and depends on one person.","Incorrect. Copied wiki fragments get adapted separately in each team's code, so they drift and cannot be governed centrally."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P30","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO situations favour a monolithic context strategy over progressive loading? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An agent loop running 80 turns where each step depends mainly on recent state."},{"l":"B","t":"A knowledge base that changes several times a day."},{"l":"C","t":"A bounded task with predictable input size where retrieval latency is not worth paying."},{"l":"D","t":"A stable instruction prefix reused on every call that benefits from prompt caching."},{"l":"E","t":"A multi-turn dialogue that accumulates tool outputs turn after turn."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Monolithic earns its place for bounded, predictable tasks and stable cacheable prefixes. Long loops and accumulating conversations are where cost and attention degrade, and fast-changing sources are better retrieved.","x":["Incorrect. In a long loop each step depends mainly on recent state, so carrying everything is costly and degrades attention. Progressive loading suits it better.","Incorrect. A knowledge base that changes several times a day is better retrieved on demand, because a monolithic copy goes stale.","Correct. For a bounded task with a predictable input size, retrieval latency is not worth paying. Loading everything up front is simpler.","Correct. A stable instruction prefix reused on every call is what prompt caching rewards, which makes a monolithic prefix efficient.","Incorrect. Accumulating tool outputs turn after turn is where cost and attention degrade, so progressive loading or compaction fits better."],"df":"medium","ob":"Optimize context windows and manage token usage"},
{"id":"P149","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A utility's billing assistant receives one block of plain text holding its instructions, the billing policy and the customer's email. The model sometimes treats sentences in the email, such as 'waive the late fee', as instructions. Which TWO changes help most? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Put the whole prompt in capital letters, so that the instructions stand out from the other content."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the billing policy entirely, so that there is less text for the model to confuse with instructions."},{"l":"C","t":"Wrap each part in clearly named tags, such as policy and email tags."},{"l":"D","t":"State that the email is data to respond to, not instructions to follow."},{"l":"E","t":"Move the customer's email into the system prompt, so that the model reads it before the policy."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Named tags separate instructions from data and let the instructions refer to each part precisely, and saying that the email is data makes its role explicit. Capitals, moving data into the system prompt or deleting needed content do not create that separation.","x":["Incorrect. Capitals make everything shout equally.","Incorrect. The policy is needed to answer correctly.","Correct. Tags make clear where each part starts and ends.","Correct. An explicit statement tells the model how to treat the email.","Incorrect. The system prompt gives the email more authority, not less."],"df":"easy","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P151","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"What is the best division of content between the system prompt and the user turn in a support assistant?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The system prompt holds the role, stable rules, and output format; the user turn holds the request-specific content."},{"l":"B","t":"The system prompt holds the customer's message; the user turn holds the rules, so that the rules are read last."},{"l":"C","t":"Everything goes in the user turn, because the system prompt is ignored by current models."},{"l":"D","t":"Everything goes in the system prompt, including each customer's message, which is rebuilt from scratch for every single request."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The system prompt carries what is stable: role, rules, and format. The user turn carries what changes per request. That split is clearer for the model and keeps the stable part cacheable.","x":["Correct. The system prompt carries what is stable, such as role, rules and format, and the user turn carries the content that changes per request.","Incorrect. This reverses the split. Putting the customer's message in the system prompt mixes changing content with stable rules.","Incorrect. System prompts are not ignored by current models, and they are the right place for stable rules.","Incorrect. Putting each customer's message into the system prompt rebuilds it every request and loses the stable, cacheable prefix."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P153","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A telecom's support team asked the model to reason step by step before answering, and accuracy on billing disputes improved by 12 points. However, the reasoning now appears in the reply customers read, and complaints about long, confusing answers have doubled. Which TWO fixes keep the accuracy gain? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use extended thinking, which returns its reasoning in a separate block."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the step-by-step instruction, accepting the lower accuracy as the price of short, clean replies."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the model to keep its reasoning very short, so that customers do not mind reading it."},{"l":"D","t":"Show the full reasoning to customers, since it makes each answer look more trustworthy."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask for the reasoning and the answer in separate tagged sections, and show only the answer."}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Keep the accuracy gain and separate the reasoning from the answer: tagged sections that the application strips, or extended thinking, whose reasoning is returned in its own block.","x":["Correct. The reasoning arrives separately and is never shown to customers.","Incorrect. It throws away a 12-point accuracy gain.","Incorrect. Shorter reasoning may lose the accuracy and is still shown.","Incorrect. Customers are already complaining about the long answers.","Correct. The application shows only the answer section."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P154","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A publisher's assistant writes article summaries that are pasted into a print layout, so lists and headers break the page. The prompt says, 'Do not use markdown, bullet points or headers,' yet the model still sometimes produces lists. Which TWO revisions are most likely to help? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Repeat the prohibition three times at different points in the prompt, so that it is harder to miss."},{"l":"B","t":"Add a line saying that using markdown will result in a penalty for the model."},{"l":"C","t":"Write the prompt itself in prose, without lists or headers."},{"l":"D","t":"Say what to do instead, such as 'write in flowing prose paragraphs'."},{"l":"E","t":"Raise the temperature, so that the model moves away from its habit of producing lists."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Positive instructions, saying what to do, work better than prohibitions, and the prompt's own style influences the output's style. Repetition, threats and temperature changes do not address the cause.","x":["Incorrect. Repeating a prohibition keeps the focus on the thing to avoid.","Incorrect. Threats do not change how the model writes.","Correct. A prose prompt pulls the output towards prose.","Correct. A positive instruction says what to produce.","Incorrect. Higher temperature adds randomness, not prose."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P158","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A request caches a prefix made of tool definitions and a system prompt, with a breakpoint after the system prompt. Which TWO changes will cause the cached prefix to miss? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Editing the description of one tool definition."},{"l":"B","t":"Changing the user's question that follows the breakpoint."},{"l":"C","t":"Reordering two sections inside the system prompt."},{"l":"D","t":"Appending a new conversation turn after the breakpoint."},{"l":"E","t":"Sending the next request with a different customer's message."}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"A cache hit needs an identical prefix up to the breakpoint. Editing a tool definition or reordering the system prompt changes that prefix. Changes after the breakpoint do not affect the cached part.","x":["Correct. Editing a tool definition changes the cached prefix, so the cache misses.","Incorrect. The user's question follows the breakpoint, so changing it does not affect the cached prefix.","Correct. Reordering sections inside the system prompt changes the prefix, so the cache misses.","Incorrect. A turn appended after the breakpoint is outside the cached prefix.","Incorrect. A different customer message falls after the breakpoint, so the cached prefix is still a hit."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P164","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's pipeline asks the model to 'return JSON' and parses the result into its case system. Roughly one response in fifty is malformed or missing fields, and each failure stops a customer case. Which TWO changes make the output structure reliable? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the model more firmly to return valid JSON, and put 'IMPORTANT' before the instruction."},{"l":"B","t":"Parse the response with a lenient parser that fills in any missing fields with its best guess."},{"l":"C","t":"Define the output with a JSON schema, through structured outputs or tool use."},{"l":"D","t":"Lower `max_tokens`, so that the model has less room to produce malformed text."},{"l":"E","t":"Validate each response against the schema before use, and route failures for handling."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"A schema enforced through structured outputs or tool use, plus validation, makes structure reliable. Firmer wording and lenient parsing leave the failure in place or hide it.","x":["Incorrect. Firmer wording lowers the failure rate at best.","Incorrect. Guessed fields put wrong data into customer cases.","Correct. A schema makes the required structure part of the request.","Incorrect. Less room makes truncated, invalid JSON more likely.","Correct. Validation catches anything that still does not fit."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P168","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"yn","q":"A retailer builds the prompts for 12 assistants from shared modules: a tone module, a safety module and task-specific modules. The safety module sits near the top of every prompt and is cached. For each statement about this design, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["A shared rule can be changed in one place.","A change to a shared module affects every prompt that uses it.","Changing a module near the top of the prompt can invalidate cached prefixes.","Modules remove the need for evaluation.","Each team should be free to edit the shared modules without review.","Module changes should be versioned and run through the evaluation like any release."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No","Yes"],"r":"Modules let a shared rule change once, but that change ripples into every prompt that uses it and can invalidate cached prefixes. Version module changes and run them through the evaluation.","x":["Yes. One edit updates every prompt that uses the module.","Yes. The reach of a change is the same as its benefit.","Yes. A changed prefix is a cache miss until it is rebuilt.","No. Every assembled prompt still needs testing.","No. An unreviewed edit can break all 12 assistants.","Yes. A shared module change is a release for every assistant."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P170","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An airline's 8,000-token system prompt has grown over a year as each team added its own rules. Critical safety rules buried in the middle are sometimes missed, and nobody is sure which older sections still matter. Which TWO actions should the architect take? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Restructure the prompt into clear sections, with critical constraints placed prominently."},{"l":"B","t":"Add each critical rule again at the end of the prompt, and leave the rest of the prompt unchanged."},{"l":"C","t":"Move the whole prompt into the user turn, so that the model reads it more closely."},{"l":"D","t":"Move to a larger model, so that it reads the long prompt more carefully and skips fewer buried rules."},{"l":"E","t":"Remove redundant text, and confirm with the evaluation that behaviour holds."}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Long prompts accumulate redundancy that hides key rules. Restructure, make critical constraints prominent, prune, and confirm with the evaluation.","x":["Correct. Clear sections and prominent constraints make critical rules hard to miss.","Incorrect. Repetition adds length and leaves the clutter in place.","Incorrect. The user turn gives the rules less authority, not more.","Incorrect. A larger model still faces the same cluttered prompt.","Correct. Pruning removes noise, and the evaluation proves nothing broke."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P36","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer's analytics assistant queries the data warehouse through a shared service account with broad read access. In an audit, a junior analyst was found to have obtained sales figures for a region they are not cleared to see. Which TWO changes fix the root cause? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a system-prompt line forbidding the assistant from mentioning any restricted region in its answers."},{"l":"B","t":"Replace the shared service account with per-user, delegated credentials."},{"l":"C","t":"Add a caching layer in front of the warehouse, so that the shared account is under less load."},{"l":"D","t":"Add output screening that blocks any response containing the word 'restricted' before it is shown."},{"l":"E","t":"Enforce the user's permissions at the data layer, before any content reaches Claude."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"A Claude layer that bypasses the underlying authorization model gives users data through a path never designed to enforce policy. Fix it where access is decided, with per-user credentials and data-layer enforcement, not in prompt wording.","x":["Incorrect. The model can be persuaded, and the data is still retrieved.","Correct. Delegated credentials carry the analyst's own access.","Incorrect. Load is not the problem; authorization is.","Incorrect. Word filters miss the data and block innocent answers.","Correct. Data-layer checks refuse content the user may not see."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P38","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's chat assistant has a p95 latency target of 3 seconds, set from what customers tolerate in a live chat. Enabling extended thinking on every request improves accuracy on complex questions but pushes p95 to 6 seconds. Which TWO configuration decisions are right? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the p95 target to 6 seconds, so that the SLA is met by the configuration as it now stands."},{"l":"B","t":"Keep the 3-second target, and measure each class of request against it separately."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep extended thinking on for every request, because accuracy always outranks latency in chat."},{"l":"D","t":"Disable extended thinking everywhere without testing, so that p95 returns to the target at once."},{"l":"E","t":"Use extended thinking only for the request classes where the evaluation shows the gain justifies the latency."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Accuracy and latency trade off, and the decision should be justified with evidence per request class. Moving the SLA to fit the configuration inverts the logic: latency targets come from what users expect.","x":["Incorrect. The target comes from customers, not from the configuration.","Correct. Per-class measurement shows where thinking fits within the target.","Incorrect. A chat that customers abandon loses the accuracy gain anyway.","Incorrect. Removing it untested gives up accuracy where it is worth the time.","Correct. Thinking goes only where the evidence shows it pays for its latency."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P39","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer wants to re-summarise 80,000 archived product reviews to populate a new catalogue. The job can run overnight and the results are needed the next morning. Which TWO considerations are correct? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The batch discount and the maximum requests per batch should be confirmed against current published pricing before the cost model is fixed."},{"l":"B","t":"Batch processing lowers the quality of the output in exchange for the discount that it provides."},{"l":"C","t":"An overnight job cannot use prompt caching, so the stable instruction block has to be removed from each request."},{"l":"D","t":"The asynchronous deadline makes the Batch API a candidate for a substantial reduction in cost."},{"l":"E","t":"Batch requests need a larger context window than standard calls, so long reviews have to be split first."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Where the SLA permits asynchronous processing, the Batch API is a cost alternative worth modelling, with its discount rate and batch size limit verified against current pricing rather than assumed. Batch does not change output quality or the context window, and call volume remains an input to the cost model.","x":["Correct. The batch discount and the batch size limits should be checked against current published pricing before the cost model is fixed.","Incorrect. Batch processing does not lower output quality. The trade-off is that results are asynchronous.","Incorrect. Prompt caching can still be used with an overnight job, so the stable block does not need to be removed.","Correct. A job that can wait overnight makes the Batch API a candidate for a substantial cost reduction.","Incorrect. Batch requests do not need a larger context window than standard calls."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P41","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's support agents paste customers' national ID numbers into messages sent to Claude, and the application's request logs now hold thousands of them in plaintext. The data protection officer wants the exposure ended and not repeated. Which TWO changes should the architect make? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask Claude in the system prompt to ignore national ID numbers and never repeat them in replies."},{"l":"B","t":"Redact before anything is logged, and purge the plaintext already in the logs."},{"l":"C","t":"Encrypt the log disk, and keep the plaintext ID numbers in the prompts so that agents work as before."},{"l":"D","t":"Rotate the application's log files more often, so that the plaintext numbers are kept for less time."},{"l":"E","t":"Redact or tokenise ID numbers on the server before the API call, passing reference IDs instead."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Anything placed in the request becomes part of the API call and any application logging. Decide which fields the context really needs and redact before the call and before logging; cleaning logs afterwards is the most expensive fix.","x":["Incorrect. The numbers are still sent and logged.","Correct. Logs never receive the numbers, and the existing exposure is removed.","Incorrect. Encryption at rest leaves plaintext in prompts and log contents.","Incorrect. Shorter retention still stores every number for a while.","Correct. The numbers never reach the API call."],"df":"medium","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P42","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"match","q":"A consultancy is building retrieval over six of its clients' document collections. The architect chooses the chunking approach by the shape of each source. Match each collection to the chunking approach that fits it best. An approach can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hierarchical chunking that keeps sections and subsections"},{"l":"B","t":"One chunk per natural unit, such as an article or an FAQ entry"},{"l":"C","t":"Chunks with document context added, such as title, company and period"}],"st":["400-page regulatory manuals, where a clause's meaning depends on its parent section.","A help centre of short, self-contained FAQ entries.","Quarterly reports, where 'revenue grew 3%' means nothing without the company and period.","Contracts with numbered clauses and sub-clauses that refer to each other.","A catalogue of one-paragraph product descriptions.","Board minutes, where every page refers to 'the company' and 'this quarter'."],"a":["A","B","C","A","B","C"],"r":"Chunking is chosen by the structure of the source. Structured documents where section context carries meaning call for hierarchical chunking; self-contained items are chunked one per item; and chunks that lose their meaning out of context need document context added.","x":["Hierarchical. A clause needs its parent section to be read correctly.","Natural unit. Each FAQ entry already stands alone.","Added context. The company and period must travel with each chunk.","Hierarchical. Numbered clauses and sub-clauses form a structure to keep.","Natural unit. Each description is one complete item.","Added context. 'The company' and 'this quarter' must be made explicit."],"df":"easy","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P43","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Users query a parts catalog with codes such as 'HX-4471-B' and also with phrases like 'the connector that keeps rattling'. Dense retrieval misses exact-code queries. Which indexing strategy fits?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Dense-only retrieval with a larger embedding model, which will learn to match part codes exactly."},{"l":"B","t":"Dense-only retrieval over 50-token fixed chunks, so that each code sits in a very small chunk."},{"l":"C","t":"Hybrid retrieval: dense embeddings for phrasing plus sparse keyword matching (BM25) for exact codes."},{"l":"D","t":"Sparse-only keyword retrieval, dropping semantic search because it cannot match part codes."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Indexing is chosen by the query pattern. Mixed queries, the common production case, need both semantic and exact-term matching, which hybrid combines.","x":["Incorrect. A larger embedding model will not reliably match exact part codes, which dense retrieval handles poorly.","Incorrect. Small chunks do not make dense vectors match an exact code. They also lose context.","Correct. Hybrid retrieval combines dense embeddings for phrases with keyword matching such as BM25 for exact codes.","Incorrect. Sparse-only retrieval handles codes but fails on phrases like 'the connector that keeps rattling'."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P46","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A company wants an assistant inside its multi-tenant web app that works over multiple turns with tools while the application controls the surrounding workflow. Which build-time choice fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A claude.ai Team plan with shared Projects, giving every customer a seat in the organisation."},{"l":"B","t":"The Claude Agent SDK, which provides a managed agent loop to embed inside the application."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code as the backend for all customers, since it already runs the agent loop for developers."},{"l":"D","t":"A single Messages API call per user message with no tools, orchestrated entirely by the front end."}],"a":["B"],"r":"The Agent SDK exists for embedding a multi-turn agent loop inside a partner's own product. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend, and claude.ai is an end-user product.","x":["Incorrect. claude.ai Team is an end-user product with seats, not a way to build an assistant inside your own multi-tenant application.","Correct. The Agent SDK provides a managed multi-turn agent loop with tools to embed inside your own application, while the application controls the workflow.","Incorrect. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend for customers.","Incorrect. A single call per message with no tools does not give a multi-turn assistant with tools, and pushes orchestration to the front end."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P49","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A manufacturer runs its ML platform, billing, and audit on Google Cloud and wants one billing and audit entry point across foundation models for a report-generation application. Which delivery route and deciding factor fit best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Vertex AI, because billing, authentication, and audit already sit in the firm's GCP environment."},{"l":"B","t":"claude.ai Team, because it needs no integration work and can be rolled out immediately."},{"l":"C","t":"AWS Bedrock, because it is the most widely used route and has the broadest customer base."},{"l":"D","t":"The direct Anthropic API, because it always has the newest features regardless of procurement."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The delivery route follows the partner's existing infrastructure and agreements. Vertex keeps identity, billing, and audit in the existing cloud, whereas Bedrock would cross clouds and claude.ai is an end-user product, not a programmatic route.","x":["Correct. The delivery route follows existing infrastructure. Vertex AI keeps billing, authentication and audit inside the firm's GCP environment.","Incorrect. claude.ai Team is an end-user product, not a programmatic route for a report-generation application, and it would not give one audit entry point across foundation models.","Incorrect. Popularity is not a deciding factor, and Bedrock would cross clouds for a firm whose billing and audit sit on Google Cloud.","Incorrect. Having the newest features does not outweigh procurement and audit needs, and the direct API would sit outside the firm's cloud billing and audit."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P51","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team proposes adding a second retrieval stage and a re-ranking model to lift answer quality in a RAG assistant. Which TWO consequences must be weighed before adopting it? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Each added stage adds retrieval latency to every request, which has to fit inside the response budget."},{"l":"B","t":"A longer retrieval pipeline allows live transactional state to be answered from the index."},{"l":"C","t":"Extra stages make the index immune to staleness when documents are added to or removed from the corpus."},{"l":"D","t":"There is more pipeline to keep correct as the corpus grows and changes, which is a recurring maintenance cost."},{"l":"E","t":"Re-ranking removes the need to track retrieval precision and recall as system metrics."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Every retrieval design trades off retrieval quality, latency, and maintenance. Extra stages can raise quality while adding per-request latency and more pipeline to keep correct. Retrieval precision and recall still need measuring, staleness still comes from indexing, and live state still belongs behind a tool call.","x":["Correct. Each added stage adds latency to every request, which has to fit within the response budget.","Incorrect. A longer retrieval pipeline does not make an index suitable for live transactional state, which belongs behind a tool call.","Incorrect. Extra stages do not remove staleness. That depends on how documents are indexed.","Correct. More pipeline means more to keep correct as the corpus grows and changes, which is a recurring maintenance cost.","Incorrect. Re-ranking does not remove the need to measure retrieval precision and recall."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P53","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A code-review subagent inside a repository agent currently inherits the main session's full tool set, including file writes and the deploy command. Review runs only need to read files and post comments, and the security team has asked for least privilege. Which TWO configuration changes fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the inherited tools, and read the subagent's transcripts after each run so that any misuse is caught later."},{"l":"B","t":"Give the subagent its own system prompt, focused on the review task."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the inherited tools, and add an instruction telling the subagent not to write files or deploy."},{"l":"D","t":"Give the subagent a read-only tool set scoped to reviewing."},{"l":"E","t":"Remove the subagent, and have the main session carry out the reviews with the tools it already holds."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Subagents exist partly so that bounded work can run with a restricted tool set and its own system prompt. Scoping the tools to the task removes the capability rather than instructing or monitoring around it, which is least privilege applied to tools.","x":["Incorrect. Reading transcripts finds misuse after the damage.","Correct. A focused prompt keeps the subagent on its task.","Incorrect. An instruction can be ignored; the capability is still there.","Correct. Read-only tools make writes and deploys impossible.","Incorrect. The main session holds even more tools."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate tool/agent configuration for capability bloat"},
{"id":"P186","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A manufacturer's finance team asks its assistant questions such as 'Total spend by vendor in Q3 above ₹10 lakh.' The data sits in a structured database, and some tables hold salary data that most finance users may not see. Which TWO design choices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Export the tables to text, chunk them, and answer each question through vector search over the chunks."},{"l":"B","t":"Limit the tool to the finance tables, and enforce each user's row-level access."},{"l":"C","t":"Paste the entire database export into the prompt for each question, so that every figure is available."},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the model to estimate the totals from a representative sample of rows from each table."},{"l":"E","t":"Give the model a read-only query tool over the database, with validated queries."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Aggregations over structured data need a query engine, not similarity search. A read-only, validated query tool gives exact answers, and limiting tables and enforcing row-level access keeps restricted data out of reach. Estimating from samples is not acceptable for finance.","x":["Incorrect. Similarity search cannot add up totals.","Correct. Users see only the rows they are allowed to see.","Incorrect. A full export is costly, exceeds limits and exposes restricted data.","Incorrect. Finance needs exact totals, not estimates.","Correct. A query tool computes exact answers without write access."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P189","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An assistant must cite its sources, and auditors need to trust the citations. Which TWO design elements support this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pass each retrieved passage with a stable source identifier and require answers to cite by that identifier."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the model to add citations from memory after it has written the answer, choosing the documents it thinks fit best."},{"l":"C","t":"Let the model invent plausible document titles where it cannot find one."},{"l":"D","t":"Remove citations from answers that are longer than a paragraph."},{"l":"E","t":"Check that each cited passage exists and contains the quoted text before showing the answer."}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Trusted citations need stable source IDs passed with the passages and a check that each citation exists and contains the quoted text. Citations from memory are unverified.","x":["Correct. Passing each retrieved passage with a stable source identifier and requiring citation by that identifier ties each claim to a real source.","Incorrect. Citations added from memory after the answer is written are unverified and can be wrong.","Incorrect. Inventing plausible titles produces fake citations, which destroys trust.","Incorrect. Removing citations from longer answers hides sources exactly where they matter.","Correct. Checking that each cited passage exists and contains the quoted text before showing the answer makes the citations verifiable."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P190","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Employees ask questions in Tamil and Hindi, while the policy documents are in English. Retrieval quality is poor for non-English queries. What is the best approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask employees to write all queries in English."},{"l":"B","t":"Index the documents only by their titles and headings, so that the language of the query matters much less during retrieval."},{"l":"C","t":"Use a multilingual embedding model, or translate the query before retrieval, and evaluate per language."},{"l":"D","t":"Increase the number of retrieved passages for non-English queries only."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Cross-language retrieval needs multilingual embeddings or query translation, with quality evaluated per language. Forcing users into English shifts the problem to them.","x":["Incorrect. Forcing employees to write in English shifts the problem onto users and loses the benefit of the assistant.","Incorrect. Indexing only titles and headings throws away most of the content and does not fix cross-language matching.","Correct. A multilingual embedding model, or translating the query before retrieval, handles the language gap, and evaluation per language shows if it works.","Incorrect. Retrieving more passages for non-English queries adds noise and does not fix poor matching."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P192","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"match","q":"A software company is building one MCP server for its support assistant. The architect wants each capability exposed through the MCP primitive built for it. Match each capability to the primitive that fits it. A primitive can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tool"},{"l":"B","t":"Resource"},{"l":"C","t":"Prompt"}],"st":["A read-only product catalogue that the client includes as context.","Creating a support ticket in the issue tracker.","A reusable template for writing an incident summary.","Looking up an order's live status by its ID.","The company's style guide, read into context when drafting replies.","A standard 'weekly account review' request that users pick from a menu."],"a":["B","A","C","A","B","C"],"r":"MCP servers expose tools (actions and live lookups), resources (data to read into context) and prompts (reusable templates).","x":["Resource. Read-only reference material is read into context.","Tool. Creating a ticket is an action.","Prompt. A reusable template is a prompt.","Tool. A live lookup with an argument is called as a tool.","Resource. The style guide is reference data for context.","Prompt. A menu of standard requests is what prompts are for."],"df":"easy","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P195","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An internal web application needs Claude to call one internal pricing service. No other client will use this tool. Which integration is the simplest adequate choice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Build an MCP server for the pricing service, so that it can be reused straight away if other clients appear in the future."},{"l":"B","t":"Build an agent-to-agent interface so that the pricing service becomes its own agent."},{"l":"C","t":"Define the tool directly in the application's API requests and call the service from application code."},{"l":"D","t":"Run Claude Code on the server and let it call the pricing service through the shell."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Choose the lightest mechanism that meets the need. With one client and one service, a tool defined in the application's own requests is simplest. MCP's value is reuse across clients.","x":["Incorrect. MCP is valuable for reuse across clients, and building a server for a single client now is extra work with no present benefit.","Incorrect. Turning a pricing service into its own agent is unnecessary for a single lookup.","Correct. With one client and one service, define the tool directly in the application's API requests and call the service from application code.","Incorrect. Running Claude Code on the server to use the shell is heavy and unsuited to a simple application call."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P196","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An insurer's chat assistant takes about eight seconds to produce a full answer, and analytics show that many users abandon the chat before it appears. The team has already cut total time as far as it can without hurting quality. Which TWO changes help most? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise `max_tokens`, so that the answer is always complete by the time it arrives."},{"l":"B","t":"Track time to first token as well as total time, since the first is what users notice."},{"l":"C","t":"Show a spinner, and send the full answer only once it is complete."},{"l":"D","t":"Stream the response, so that users see text within the first second or two."},{"l":"E","t":"Cut the answer to a single sentence for every question, whatever is asked."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Streaming cuts the time to first token, which is what users perceive. Total time is unchanged, but abandonment drops, and measuring time to first token shows the improvement.","x":["Incorrect. A larger output limit does not make anything appear sooner.","Correct. Time to first token is the measure that matches what users feel.","Incorrect. A spinner still leaves eight seconds of nothing to read.","Correct. Text appears almost at once, so users stay.","Incorrect. One sentence cannot answer every question."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P197","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer's returns assistant performs a policy lookup (1.5 s), a customer lookup (1.2 s), and then a model call (3 s) for each request. The two lookups are independent of each other. The p95 target is 5 s, and it currently runs at 6.2 s. Which TWO changes target the problem? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run the two independent lookups in parallel, so that the slower one, not their sum, sets the wait."},{"l":"B","t":"Move to a larger model tier, on the basis that a more capable model finishes its part faster."},{"l":"C","t":"Measure each step's latency at p95, so that the slowest step can be found and targeted."},{"l":"D","t":"Add a second model call to check the first answer before replying to the customer."},{"l":"E","t":"Raise the p95 target to 7 s, so that the current design passes its target."}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Independent calls should run in parallel so that the wait is set by the slowest, here about 1.5 s plus 3 s. Measuring each step shows where the time goes. Moving the target or adding calls does not fix the design.","x":["Correct. Parallel lookups bring the total to about 4.5 seconds.","Incorrect. A larger tier is usually slower, not faster.","Correct. Per-step figures show which step to work on.","Incorrect. Another call adds more time.","Incorrect. Moving the target hides the problem."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P198","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Claude returns several tool-use requests in one response, and they are independent of each other. Which TWO practices are correct for the application? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Execute only the first request and ignore the rest."},{"l":"B","t":"Execute the independent requests concurrently where it is safe to do so."},{"l":"C","t":"Send each result back in its own separate API call, one after another."},{"l":"D","t":"Return all the results together in the next message, each matched to its request."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the user to choose which request should run."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"When Claude requests several independent tools, run them concurrently where safe and return all results together, each matched to its request.","x":["Incorrect. Ignoring the other independent requests leaves part of Claude's request unanswered, so the task cannot be completed correctly.","Correct. Independent requests can run concurrently where it is safe, which saves time.","Incorrect. Sending each result in its own separate call is not how results are returned. They go back together in the next message.","Correct. Return all the results together in the next message, each matched to its request.","Incorrect. The user should not have to choose which request runs. Claude asked for all of them."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P202","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer's product assistant has shown normal latency and error rates for three months. A spot check before a board meeting finds that many answers are fluent and wrong, and nobody knows when the problem started. Which TWO monitoring additions would have caught it? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Track the quality scores over time, with an alert when they drop."},{"l":"B","t":"More frequent CPU and memory monitoring on the servers that host the application."},{"l":"C","t":"A higher error-rate threshold, so that alerts are no longer sent for small changes."},{"l":"D","t":"Grade a sample of production answers with a calibrated judge or people."},{"l":"E","t":"Network latency monitoring between the application and the API, since slow connections cause wrong answers."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Operational metrics cannot see wrong-but-fluent answers. Sampled quality grading of production outputs, tracked over time with alerts, catches silent quality failure.","x":["Correct. A trend with an alert shows when quality started to fall.","Incorrect. Server load says nothing about whether answers are right.","Incorrect. Fewer alerts hide more, and errors were not the issue.","Correct. Grading real answers measures quality directly.","Incorrect. Network speed does not make answers wrong."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze observability challenges and select monitoring strategies at scale"},
{"id":"P206","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"yn","q":"An engineering company's assistant used to load all 200 reference manuals up front. To cut cost and noise, the team is moving to progressive discovery, where the assistant sees short descriptions and loads a manual only when needed. For each statement about the change, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["The assistant may fail to find or load a manual it needs.","Each load adds a step, which can add latency.","Clear, specific descriptions help the assistant load the right manual.","Evaluations should check that the right material is found, not only that final answers are good.","Progressive discovery stops prompt caching from working on any part of the request.","Progressive discovery always costs more tokens than loading everything up front."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No"],"r":"Progressive discovery trades context size for the risk that needed material is not found, plus an extra step for each load. Good descriptions and evaluations that check discovery manage the risk.","x":["Yes. A manual that is never loaded cannot be used.","Yes. Each load is another step before the answer.","Yes. The descriptions are what the assistant chooses from.","Yes. Discovery itself needs testing.","No. The stable part of the prompt can still be cached.","No. It usually costs less, since most manuals are never loaded."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate progressive discovery vs. monolithic context strategy"},
{"id":"P207","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A law firm's document-analysis job takes several minutes per request. The current design holds an HTTP connection open from the user's browser until the job finishes, many requests time out, and the firm expects upload volume to triple at quarter-end. Which TWO design changes fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise every timeout in the chain, from the browser to the load balancer and the backend, to thirty minutes."},{"l":"B","t":"Split each document into single pages and process the pages in the user's browser."},{"l":"C","t":"Accept the job, return a job ID, process it asynchronously, and notify the user or let them poll."},{"l":"D","t":"Process jobs from a queue, so that a burst of uploads does not overload the backend."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask users to submit only short documents, so that each job finishes before the connection times out."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Long-running work should be asynchronous: accept, return a job ID, process, and notify or allow polling, with a queue to absorb bursts. Long synchronous timeouts are fragile.","x":["Incorrect. Thirty-minute connections are fragile and tie up resources.","Incorrect. Splitting in the browser loses context across pages.","Correct. The browser no longer waits on an open connection.","Correct. A queue smooths the quarter-end burst.","Incorrect. The firm's documents are long by nature."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P209","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"A research agent can browse the web and send emails on the user's behalf. Which THREE controls are appropriate? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Treat web page content as untrusted input that can contain injected instructions."},{"l":"B","t":"Trust content from sites that use HTTPS, since encryption shows that the source is safe."},{"l":"C","t":"Require user confirmation before any email is sent."},{"l":"D","t":"Give the agent the user's full account credentials so that it never has to ask."},{"l":"E","t":"Restrict which recipients or domains the email tool can send to."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"Web content is untrusted, so side-effecting actions need confirmation and a scope limit. HTTPS secures transport, not content, and full credentials maximise the damage an injection can do.","x":["Correct. Web page content is untrusted input and can contain injected instructions, so it must not be treated as commands.","Incorrect. HTTPS protects the connection, not the content, so a page served over HTTPS can still contain an injection.","Correct. Requiring user confirmation before any email is sent puts a gate in front of a side-effecting action.","Incorrect. Full credentials maximise the damage an injected instruction can do, so the agent should have only the access it needs.","Correct. Restricting recipients or domains limits where a manipulated agent could send information."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P210","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A CRM tool returns full customer records, including national ID numbers and bank details, although the agent only needs name, plan, and open tickets. What is the best change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the full record and ask the model not to mention sensitive fields."},{"l":"B","t":"Encrypt the full record in the tool output so that the model cannot read it."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the full record but delete conversation logs after a week."},{"l":"D","t":"Change the tool so that it returns only the fields the agent's task needs."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Data minimisation at the tool boundary keeps sensitive fields out of context and logs altogether. Prompt instructions and short retention do not.","x":["Incorrect. A prompt instruction does not remove the sensitive fields from context, logs or the API request.","Incorrect. Encrypted output the model cannot read would also be unusable for the task, and the fields the agent needs still have to be readable.","Incorrect. Deleting logs after a week leaves the sensitive fields in context and logs for that week, and does not minimise exposure.","Correct. Data minimisation at the tool boundary returns only the fields the task needs, so sensitive data never reaches context or logs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P211","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"match","q":"A logistics company is connecting its agents to six systems. The architect wants the simplest integration that is reliable for each. Match each system to the integration that fits it best. An integration can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A thin service that wraps it as a typed tool, with validation"},{"l":"B","t":"Its existing command-line interface"},{"l":"C","t":"An MCP server shared by several clients"},{"l":"D","t":"A tool that calls its documented REST API"}],"st":["A legacy order system that only accepts complex XML messages over SOAP.","Developer tools such as git and kubectl, which have mature CLIs, used from Claude Code.","A ticket tool needed by Claude.ai, Claude Code and an internal web app.","A modern inventory service with a documented JSON API, used by one application.","A mainframe billing system reachable only through fixed-format messages.","A product-search tool used by two internal assistants and by Claude Code."],"a":["A","B","C","D","A","C"],"r":"Wrap complex legacy protocols in a thin service with a simple typed tool schema and validation, so that the model fills in a few fields and code builds the message. Use mature CLIs as they are, share a tool across clients through MCP, and call a clean API directly.","x":["Thin service. Code builds the XML; the model supplies a few typed fields.","CLI. Mature command-line tools are the simplest integration.","MCP server. One server serves all three clients.","REST tool. A clean API for one application needs only a tool.","Thin service. Fixed-format messages are built reliably in code.","MCP server. Several clients share one implementation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P55","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A startup plans to build its claims-triage workflow first and 'add tests once it looks right'. Which recommendation best reflects good practice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Build the prototype first, because the team needs a working system to know what is worth measuring."},{"l":"B","t":"Define success criteria, a golden dataset, and graders before production code so that evals gate every change."},{"l":"C","t":"Rely on a stakeholder demo as the acceptance test, since stakeholders know what good looks like."},{"l":"D","t":"Launch and use user complaints as the dataset, because real feedback is the most representative."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Writing evals first forces success to be stated in measurable terms, exposes assumptions while they are cheap to change, and creates a gate for every model swap, prompt change, or retrieval change.","x":["Incorrect. Building first leaves success undefined. Measurable criteria written first expose assumptions while they are cheap to change.","Correct. Success criteria, a golden dataset and graders defined before production code let evals gate every prompt, model or retrieval change.","Incorrect. A demo is not a test. It shows hand-picked cases and does not give repeatable acceptance criteria.","Incorrect. Using complaints as the dataset means users find the failures first, and the sample is biased toward the cases that were reported."],"df":"easy","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P57","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Which THREE practices make an LLM-as-judge more reliable? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Grade with a different model than the one that produced the outputs."},{"l":"B","t":"Use a detailed rubric with a small fixed set of verdict labels."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask for a free-form 1-100 score with no reasoning."},{"l":"D","t":"Calibrate the judge against human-labelled examples."},{"l":"E","t":"Use the same model that generated the answers to keep grading consistent."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"Rigorous judging uses detailed rubrics, constrained verdicts, calibration against human labels, and a different grader to avoid self-preference. Free-form scores without reasoning hide inconsistency in borderline cases.","x":["Correct. A judge from a different model avoids self-preference, because a model tends to favour its own outputs.","Correct. A detailed rubric with a small fixed set of verdict labels makes judgements consistent and comparable.","Incorrect. A free-form 1-100 score without reasoning hides inconsistency in borderline cases and is hard to calibrate.","Correct. Calibrating the judge against human-labelled examples shows where it disagrees with people and lets you correct it.","Incorrect. The same model that wrote the answers tends to favour its own style, so it is a weaker grader."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P58","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A travel company's summarisation feature scores 97% on its evaluation set, yet users report failures on long, messy, multilingual booking emails. The evaluation set contains only short, clean English samples. Which TWO fixes are most appropriate? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add the failing user cases, with expected outputs, to the evaluation set."},{"l":"B","t":"Raise the pass threshold on the existing dataset to 99%, so that weaker results are rejected."},{"l":"C","t":"Set the failing user cases aside as outliers that do not represent normal use."},{"l":"D","t":"Rescore the same dataset with a model judge in place of the current grading function."},{"l":"E","t":"Rebuild the dataset from real inputs, including long, messy and multilingual ones."}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"If the dataset is not representative, its scores are not meaningful. The golden dataset must include the edge cases and the real inputs the system meets, starting with the failures users have reported.","x":["Correct. Known failures become tests that future changes must pass.","Incorrect. A higher threshold on the wrong data still measures the wrong thing.","Incorrect. Users' real inputs are what the system must handle.","Incorrect. A new grader on the same narrow data has the same blind spot.","Correct. A representative dataset makes the score meaningful."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P59","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A prompt change raises the average eval score from 82 to 85, but the 'adversarial' category falls from 78 to 61. What should the team conclude?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Drop the adversarial category from the eval, since it is small and drags the results down."},{"l":"B","t":"Not clearly an improvement; review per-category results against the shipping criteria before deciding."},{"l":"C","t":"Rerun the eval repeatedly until the adversarial score recovers to its earlier level."},{"l":"D","t":"Ship the change, because the mean score improved and the mean is the headline metric."}],"a":["B"],"r":"A change that raises the mean while quietly degrading edge cases or adversarial inputs does not make the system better. Interpret per-category results, not just the aggregate.","x":["Incorrect. Dropping the adversarial category hides the regression instead of dealing with it.","Correct. The mean improved but the adversarial category fell sharply, so review per-category results against the shipping criteria before deciding.","Incorrect. Rerunning until the score recovers is selecting the result you want and not evidence.","Incorrect. A higher mean does not justify shipping when a category has degraded, because the mean can hide that."],"df":"medium","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P60","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A lender's team evaluated a model swap for its document assistant and saw a 4-point drop on the golden dataset. After seeing the result, it announced that a 5-point drop is 'acceptable' and scheduled the release. The risk team objects. Which TWO practices should the team adopt? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Block any model swap that shows any drop at all, however small, on any category."},{"l":"B","t":"Set the acceptance threshold before running the evaluation, not after seeing the result."},{"l":"C","t":"Stop using the golden dataset to compare models, since it cannot compare two models fairly."},{"l":"D","t":"Skip the evaluation whenever the vendor states that the newer model is better."},{"l":"E","t":"Treat each model swap as a release that ships only if it meets the threshold set in advance."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Set the threshold before running the evaluation. A threshold chosen afterwards rationalises the outcome rather than gating it, so each swap is a release against a bar agreed in advance.","x":["Incorrect. Some variation is normal; the point is to agree the bar first.","Correct. A bar set first cannot be bent to fit the result.","Incorrect. Golden datasets are exactly how models are compared.","Incorrect. A vendor's claim is not evidence about this task.","Correct. Gating the release on the agreed bar makes the decision honest."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct A/B testing and iterative improvements"},
{"id":"P62","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"After two days of an A/B test of a new prompt for an insurer's assistant, the treatment is ahead, and the product owner wants to stop and ship. Traffic is modest, and model outputs vary from run to run. Which TWO practices should the team follow? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stop as soon as the difference first looks statistically significant, to limit exposure to the old prompt."},{"l":"B","t":"Work out the sample size in advance from the baseline, minimum detectable effect and confidence."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep running until the result matches the hypothesis the team wrote down at the start."},{"l":"D","t":"Run the test until that sample size is reached, whatever the early results show."},{"l":"E","t":"Skip the rest of the test, because an early lead in a short window reliably proves the effect."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"A team that runs until it sees what it wants will find it, whether or not it is real. Sample size comes from the minimum detectable effect, the baseline and the required confidence, and the test runs to it.","x":["Incorrect. Stopping at the first significant moment inflates false positives.","Correct. A sample size fixed in advance protects against early noise.","Incorrect. Running until it matches the hope guarantees a biased result.","Correct. Running to the planned size gives the result its confidence.","Incorrect. Two days of variable outputs prove nothing."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct A/B testing and iterative improvements"},
{"id":"P65","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's team switched its statement-summary feature to a cheaper model tier without re-evaluating. Format-compliance failures rose from 1% to 9% that week, with no prompt changes and no change to the input. Which TWO actions fit the failure? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add exponential backoff, so that failed responses are retried until a valid one comes back."},{"l":"B","t":"Rewrite the prompt from scratch, since it must always have been ambiguous."},{"l":"C","t":"Make every tier change pass the evaluation gate before release."},{"l":"D","t":"Add retrieval over the format specification, so that the model can look up the required format."},{"l":"E","t":"Re-evaluate the model tiers against the golden dataset."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"The tier is wrong for the task, or was swapped without re-evaluation, so the fix is in model selection gated by an evaluation. Nothing else changed, which points away from prompt and retrieval causes.","x":["Incorrect. Retries do not fix a model that cannot follow the format.","Incorrect. The prompt did not change, and it worked before.","Correct. A gate stops the next unchecked swap.","Incorrect. The format was never missing; the model change is the cause.","Correct. Re-evaluating shows which tier meets the format requirement."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P66","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A pharmaceutical company's RAG assistant has slowly got worse over six months, while its document corpus doubled. Latency, the model version and the prompts are unchanged. The team wants to find the cause before changing anything. Which TWO investigations come first? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Lower the temperature, so that answers vary less as the corpus grows."},{"l":"B","t":"Add a content-delivery network in front of the API, in case bandwidth between regions has fallen."},{"l":"C","t":"Check that the new documents were indexed consistently, with the same chunking and metadata as the old ones."},{"l":"D","t":"Switch to a larger tier, and compare its answers with the current tier's on a sample."},{"l":"E","t":"Measure retrieval precision and recall on a labelled set of queries."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"RAG's characteristic failure is retrieval quality drift as documents are added without consistent indexing. Treat retrieval precision and recall as system metrics, and check how the new documents were indexed.","x":["Incorrect. Temperature has not changed and does not explain a slow decline.","Incorrect. Latency is unchanged, so bandwidth is not the issue.","Correct. Inconsistent indexing of new documents is the likeliest cause.","Incorrect. The model did not change, so it is not the first suspect.","Correct. Retrieval metrics show whether the right passages still come back."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P68","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team's demo shows a 1.2-second median response time. The product SLA is user-facing and the workload will be concurrent. Which latency measure should anchor the target?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The average of the fastest half of requests, because it removes outliers that distort the picture."},{"l":"B","t":"Median latency measured in single-request demos, because it shows the typical user experience."},{"l":"C","t":"p95 under realistic concurrent load, because the slow tail drives SLA breaches and abandonment."},{"l":"D","t":"The minimum observed latency, because it shows what the system can achieve when everything works."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Latency p95 under concurrent load is a different number from median latency under a single request, and the tail is what users and SLAs feel.","x":["Incorrect. Averaging the fastest half removes the slow tail, which is what users and SLAs feel.","Incorrect. A median from single-request demos does not reflect concurrent load or the slow tail.","Correct. p95 under realistic concurrent load captures the slow tail that causes SLA breaches and abandonment.","Incorrect. The minimum shows the best case only and says nothing about what most users experience."],"df":"easy","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P70","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"match","q":"An insurer's claims system extracts claim fields, summarises the claim narrative for an adjuster, and must never deny a claim on its own. The architect is designing its evaluation and wants the cheapest reliable method for each behaviour. Match each behaviour to its method. A method can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Code check"},{"l":"B","t":"Calibrated model judge"},{"l":"C","t":"Human review"}],"st":["The system never takes a deny action on a claim.","The extracted claim fields match the labelled values.","The narrative summary is faithful to the adjuster's notes.","The summary's tone is neutral and professional.","Calibrating the faithfulness judge before the team relies on it.","A new kind of claim that the rubric does not yet cover."],"a":["A","A","B","B","C","C"],"r":"Use the cheapest reliable method per behaviour: binary and structured checks in code, interpretive judgements such as faithfulness with a calibrated judge, and people for calibration and for cases nobody has defined yet.","x":["Code. Whether a deny action happened is binary.","Code. Field values are compared exactly.","Model judge. Faithfulness needs interpretation.","Model judge. Tone is an open-ended language quality.","Human review. People provide the labels the judge is calibrated against.","Human review. A new case needs judgement before it can be automated."],"df":"medium","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P214","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A software company wants a model judge to compare two prompt versions for its documentation assistant, whose answers are open-ended. Earlier experiments gave results that flipped depending on how the judge was asked. Which TWO design choices make the judging reliable? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Compare the two answers side by side against a written rubric, rather than scoring each alone."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the judge to give each answer an absolute score from 1 to 100, with no explanation."},{"l":"C","t":"Always show version A first and version B second, so that the order stays consistent."},{"l":"D","t":"Randomise or swap the order of the two answers, so that position bias cancels out."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the judge which answer is longer, since length is a fair proxy for completeness."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Pairwise comparison against a rubric is more reliable than absolute scores, and swapping or randomising the order controls position bias. Length is not quality.","x":["Correct. Side-by-side comparison against criteria gives steadier verdicts.","Incorrect. Absolute scores without reasons drift and are hard to trust.","Incorrect. A fixed order lets position bias favour one version.","Correct. Swapping the order removes position bias from the result.","Incorrect. Longer answers are not better answers."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P218","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"yn","q":"A support assistant's evaluation scores unusually high, and the team is ready to celebrate. A reviewer notices that several evaluation cases are word-for-word copies of the few-shot examples in the prompt. For each statement, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["The score overstates real performance, because the model is tested on answers it was shown.","Evaluation cases should be kept separate from the examples in the prompt.","The copied cases should be replaced with new cases the prompt has not seen.","Some overlap is harmless in a large evaluation set.","A stricter judge from another model family would correct the score.","The few-shot examples are too short, which is why the model copies them."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],"r":"If evaluation cases appear in the prompt, the model is being tested on answers it was shown. Keep evaluation data separate from prompt examples.","x":["Yes. The model has already seen these answers.","Yes. Separation is what makes the score mean anything.","Yes. Unseen cases measure real performance.","No. Leaked cases inflate the score whatever the set size.","No. The judge is not the problem; the cases are.","No. The model scores well because the answers are in the prompt."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P224","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An agent often calls `search_orders` when it should call `get_order`, even when the user gives an exact order ID. The two tool descriptions are almost identical. What is the most likely cause and fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hallucination; add retrieval so that the model knows which orders exist."},{"l":"B","t":"Model mismatch; move the agent to a larger model tier so that it chooses between similar tools more carefully on every request."},{"l":"C","t":"Overlapping tool descriptions; make each description state clearly when to use it and when to use the other."},{"l":"D","t":"Rate limiting; add backoff so that the correct tool has time to respond."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Near-identical descriptions leave the model guessing. Each description should say when to use the tool and how it differs from similar ones.","x":["Incorrect. The agent is not inventing facts. It is picking between two similar tools, so retrieval of order data does not address the cause.","Incorrect. A larger tier may help a little, but the cause is almost identical descriptions, which a bigger model also has to guess between.","Correct. Overlapping descriptions leave the model guessing. Each should state when to use it and how it differs from the similar tool.","Incorrect. Rate limiting returns errors, and does not cause the model to pick the wrong tool."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P226","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An assistant performs well in English but poorly in Hindi. The golden dataset contains only English cases, and the release passed every threshold. What is the root problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model cannot handle Hindi, so Hindi support should be withdrawn."},{"l":"B","t":"The eval did not cover a population the system serves; add a Hindi slice with its own threshold."},{"l":"C","t":"The thresholds were too low; raising them on the English set will fix Hindi quality."},{"l":"D","t":"The judge model was biased against Hindi, so it should be replaced."}],"a":["B"],"r":"An eval only tells you about the populations it contains. Add slices for every population served, each with its own threshold.","x":["Incorrect. Poor Hindi performance is not proof that the model cannot handle Hindi. The gap is that nothing in the eval measured it.","Correct. The golden dataset did not cover a population the system serves. Add a Hindi slice with its own threshold.","Incorrect. Raising thresholds on an English-only set cannot show or improve Hindi quality.","Incorrect. Nothing shows the judge is biased. With no Hindi cases, the eval had no way to detect the problem."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P228","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"yn","q":"An insurer's letter-writing assistant changed its output style overnight with no code or prompt change, and complaints started the same morning. The application refers to the model by an alias, which now points to a newer version. For each statement, select Yes if it is sound practice or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["A model alias can move to a newer version without any change in your code.","Production should pin a specific model version.","Moving to a new version should be gated on the evaluation, like any release.","Lowering the temperature makes a new version behave like the old one.","Using the alias is best, because the application always gets the latest version.","The prompt should be rewritten from scratch whenever the style changes."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],"r":"Aliases can move to new versions. Production should pin a version and treat each move as a release gated by the evaluation.","x":["Yes. The alias moved, so behaviour changed with no code change.","Yes. A pinned version changes only when the team decides.","Yes. The evaluation shows whether the new version is acceptable.","No. Temperature does not turn one version into another.","No. Unplanned changes are what caused the complaints.","No. Pinning prevents the surprise; rewriting chases it."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P229","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO changes can reduce latency without risking answer quality? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Cache a long, stable prompt prefix so that it is not reprocessed on every request."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the guardrail checks that run before the answer is shown."},{"l":"C","t":"Cut `max_tokens` below the length that a complete answer needs."},{"l":"D","t":"Run independent retrieval and tool calls in parallel instead of in sequence."},{"l":"E","t":"Skip the eval for configuration changes that affect latency only."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Caching a stable prefix and parallelising independent calls cut latency without changing what the model sees. Removing checks, truncating output, or skipping evals trade quality for speed.","x":["Correct. Caching a long stable prefix avoids reprocessing it each request, which cuts latency without changing the content.","Incorrect. Removing guardrail checks trades safety for speed.","Incorrect. Cutting max_tokens below the length of a complete answer truncates answers and hurts quality.","Correct. Running independent retrieval and tool calls in parallel means the slowest call sets the wait and not their sum.","Incorrect. Skipping the eval for latency-only changes can let a quality regression through."],"df":"hard","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P230","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A consulting firm's report-writing feature produces long, padded reports. Cost analysis shows that output tokens dominate the bill, and the partners say clients prefer shorter reports anyway, as long as the content holds. Which TWO changes target this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Check with the evaluation that quality holds at the shorter length."},{"l":"B","t":"Shorten the system prompt instead, since input tokens drive the cost of long reports in most applications."},{"l":"C","t":"Move to a larger tier, so that the reports are written more efficiently."},{"l":"D","t":"Specify a concise output format and a target length."},{"l":"E","t":"Remove prompt caching, because caching makes the outputs longer."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Output tokens are priced higher than input tokens. A concise format and target length reduce them directly, with the evaluation confirming that quality holds.","x":["Correct. The evaluation proves the shorter reports keep their content.","Incorrect. The analysis shows output tokens dominate.","Incorrect. A larger tier costs more per token.","Correct. A target length cuts the expensive output directly.","Incorrect. Caching does not lengthen outputs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P236","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to keep prompts and responses for debugging, but they may contain personal data. Which TWO practices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Store everything in plaintext with open access, so that debugging is fast."},{"l":"B","t":"Disable all logging, so that no personal data can ever be stored."},{"l":"C","t":"Redact or tokenise sensitive fields before logs are written."},{"l":"D","t":"Restrict access to the logs and apply a defined retention period."},{"l":"E","t":"Copy logs to personal laptops so that engineers can work offline."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Redact before writing, then limit access and retention. Disabling logging blinds debugging, and open or copied logs spread personal data.","x":["Incorrect. Plaintext with open access exposes personal data to anyone, which is a privacy failure.","Incorrect. Disabling all logging removes the ability to debug, which the team needs.","Correct. Redacting or tokenising sensitive fields before the logs are written keeps debugging useful while reducing exposure.","Correct. Restricted access and a defined retention period limit who can see the logs and for how long.","Incorrect. Copying logs to personal laptops spreads personal data outside controlled systems."],"df":"medium","ob":"Monitor system performance using logging and observability tools"},
{"id":"P237","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Offline evals passed, but the team wants ongoing evidence of quality in production. Which approach fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sample a share of production traffic, grade it with a calibrated judge, and track the trend against thresholds."},{"l":"B","t":"Re-run the offline eval once a quarter and assume production matches it."},{"l":"C","t":"Grade every production request with a trained person, regardless of volume, so that no wrong answer can go unnoticed."},{"l":"D","t":"Rely on the error rate, since wrong answers usually cause errors."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Sampled online grading with a calibrated judge, tracked against thresholds, gives continuous quality evidence at reasonable cost.","x":["Correct. Sample a share of production traffic, grade it with a calibrated judge and track the trend against thresholds. This gives continuous evidence at reasonable cost.","Incorrect. A quarterly offline re-run assumes production matches it and misses drift between runs.","Incorrect. Grading every production request by person does not scale and is very costly.","Incorrect. Wrong answers usually do not cause errors, so the error rate can look fine while quality drops."],"df":"hard","ob":"Monitor system performance using logging and observability tools"},
{"id":"P240","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"A release review must decide whether a new configuration ships. Which THREE items belong in the eval report? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Per-category scores compared with the thresholds agreed in advance."},{"l":"B","t":"Only the aggregate score, so that the decision is simple."},{"l":"C","t":"Comparison with the current baseline on the same dataset version."},{"l":"D","t":"Screenshots of a few good answers chosen by the team."},{"l":"E","t":"The cost and latency differences against the baseline."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"A release decision needs per-category results against pre-agreed thresholds, a like-for-like baseline comparison, and cost and latency effects. Aggregates and chosen screenshots hide regressions.","x":["Correct. Per-category scores against thresholds agreed in advance show where the change helps or hurts.","Incorrect. An aggregate-only report hides regressions in individual categories.","Correct. A comparison with the current baseline on the same dataset version shows whether the change is an improvement.","Incorrect. Screenshots of a few good answers chosen by the team are selective and not evidence.","Correct. Cost and latency differences against the baseline are part of the release decision."],"df":"hard","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P73","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"yn","q":"A wealth-management firm's assistant must never recommend products on the firm's restricted list, which compliance updates every week. A manager argues that Claude's trained behaviour already declines harmful content, so nothing more is needed. For each statement, select Yes if it is correct or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["Claude's training covers broad harms but has never seen the firm's restricted list.","The rule should be enforced in the application layer with a deterministic check.","A single reminder line in the system prompt is enough to enforce firm policy.","Every recommended product should be checked against the current list before the reply is sent.","Because training declines harmful content, the firm need not enforce anything itself.","A rule this specific cannot be enforced by software at all."],"a":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes","No","No"],"r":"Training reduces broad classes of harm; the firm's own policy, data rules and authorization are the firm's to enforce. The dangerous failure is silent: assuming Claude enforces a rule it was never given.","x":["Yes. Training cannot know one firm's weekly list.","Yes. A code check applies the rule every time.","No. A reminder can be ignored or argued around.","Yes. Checking against the current list catches products added this week.","No. The firm owns its domain rules.","No. A list comparison is exactly what software does reliably."],"df":"easy","ob":"Implement guardrails and safety controls"},
{"id":"P78","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Attackers submit enormous, padded documents that inflate cost and cause responses to truncate. Which control directly addresses this risk category?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An output toxicity classifier that checks every response before it reaches the end user."},{"l":"B","t":"Input size limits, chunking, and budget monitoring with alerts on tokens per request."},{"l":"C","t":"Single sign-on for end users, so that every request can be tied to a known identity."},{"l":"D","t":"A deterministic allowlist for the tools the agent is permitted to call on behalf of users."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Token-budget exhaustion is its own risk category: oversized or padded inputs consume the context or output budget. Limits and monitoring address it; content classifiers and tool allowlists address other risks.","x":["Incorrect. A toxicity classifier addresses harmful content, not oversized padded input that inflates cost.","Correct. Input size limits, chunking and budget monitoring with alerts on tokens per request directly address token-budget exhaustion.","Incorrect. Single sign-on ties requests to an identity but does not limit how big a request can be.","Incorrect. A tool allowlist limits which tools can be called, which is a different risk from oversized inputs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P79","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO pairings of decision type and review placement are appropriate? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Every decision of every kind, with pre-action approval."},{"l":"B","t":"An irreversible account closure flagged at low confidence, with pre-action approval."},{"l":"C","t":"A benefit denial near the eligibility threshold, with no review because confidence is 0.92."},{"l":"D","t":"Reversible subject-line suggestions for marketing emails, with post-action audit."},{"l":"E","t":"A medication-dose recommendation, with sampled review only."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Pre-action approval suits irreversible, high-cost cases; post-action audit suits reversible, lower-cost ones. Sampled review monitors the system rather than guarding individual outcomes, high confidence does not lower the stakes of an irreversible harm, and reviewing everything overwhelms reviewers.","x":["Incorrect. Reviewing every decision before action overwhelms reviewers and slows the low-risk cases that do not need it.","Correct. An irreversible account closure flagged at low confidence needs pre-action approval, because it cannot be undone afterwards.","Incorrect. A denial near the eligibility threshold is a high-stakes decision, and high confidence does not lower the stakes. It should not go without review.","Correct. Reversible subject-line suggestions are low cost, so a post-action audit is enough.","Incorrect. Sampled review monitors the system but does not guard an individual dose recommendation, where a wrong answer can cause harm."],"df":"hard","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P80","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"To be safe, a health insurer routes every AI-drafted claim decision to human approval. Within weeks, reviewers are approving nearly everything within seconds, and an audit finds errors that were approved. Which TWO changes fix this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add an input classifier, since attackers must be steering the reviewers' decisions."},{"l":"B","t":"Route decisions to review by confidence, reversibility and cost of error, not all of them."},{"l":"C","t":"Shorten the reviewer interface, since the content on each screen is too long to read."},{"l":"D","t":"Retrain the model on the reviewers' decisions, since its behaviour has drifted."},{"l":"E","t":"Give reviewers the evidence for each decision, and track how often they overturn the AI."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Routing everything to people floods the queue until reviewers click through without reading. Focus review on the decisions that warrant it, give reviewers evidence, and watch for rubber-stamping.","x":["Incorrect. This is fatigue, not an attack.","Correct. Fewer, riskier cases get real attention.","Incorrect. A shorter screen does not fix an overwhelming queue.","Incorrect. The model is not the problem; the review design is.","Correct. Evidence helps reviewers check, and the overturn rate shows rubber-stamping."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P81","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A hospital holds a signed BAA for its first-party API configuration. A developer wants to use a beta feature with PHI. What should the architect do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Confirm that the feature is explicitly listed as BAA-eligible, since beta features are generally excluded."},{"l":"B","t":"Move the team to a consumer Claude.ai plan, which has simpler terms for handling health data."},{"l":"C","t":"Proceed with the beta feature, because a signed BAA covers every feature offered on that configuration."},{"l":"D","t":"Proceed as long as the processing region is pinned to the United States, since residency satisfies HIPAA."}],"a":["A"],"r":"BAA coverage is configuration- and feature-specific. Eligible features must be confirmed; beta features are generally excluded unless explicitly listed.","x":["Correct. BAA coverage is specific to configuration and feature. Confirm the feature is explicitly listed as eligible, because beta features are generally excluded.","Incorrect. A consumer plan does not suit PHI, and a BAA for the API configuration does not carry over to it.","Incorrect. A signed BAA does not automatically cover every feature, including beta ones.","Incorrect. Pinning the region does not by itself satisfy HIPAA, and residency does not make a beta feature eligible."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P84","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Where can unequal outcomes enter a Claude-based decision system? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Downstream routing that sends some groups down different paths."},{"l":"B","t":"Few-shot examples that carry the same skew."},{"l":"C","t":"The TLS cipher suite used to encrypt the API traffic between the application and the provider."},{"l":"D","t":"A retrieval corpus that over- or under-represents some groups."},{"l":"E","t":"The number of retries configured on the client for transient network errors."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"The four identifiable entry points are the retrieval corpus, prompt framing, few-shot examples, and downstream routing. Naming them makes fairness something you can instrument rather than assume the provider handles.","x":["Correct. Downstream routing that sends some groups down different paths can produce unequal outcomes.","Correct. Few-shot examples that carry a skew teach the same skew to the model.","Incorrect. A cipher suite protects traffic in transit and has no effect on fairness of decisions.","Correct. A retrieval corpus that over- or under-represents some groups can bias what the model sees.","Incorrect. The number of retries for network errors does not influence whether outcomes differ across groups."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P87","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A lending assistant's decisions have to be explainable. Which description of the explanations the system must support is correct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"One record serves several audiences: the applicant's reason, the regulator's reconstruction, the team's debugging."},{"l":"B","t":"One explanation written for the regulator is sufficient, since the regulator is the most demanding of the audiences."},{"l":"C","t":"Explanations can be produced on request by asking the model why it decided as it did once the decision is made."},{"l":"D","t":"Explainability is a property of the model the vendor supplies, so it is settled during model selection."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Fairness and transparency are instrumented rather than assumed. Decision logging is built once and serves the affected person, the regulator, the build team, and the control register, each taking a different explanation from the same record. Asking the model to rationalise afterwards is not a reconstruction of what happened.","x":["Correct. One decision record serves several audiences, including the applicant's reason, the regulator's reconstruction and the team's debugging.","Incorrect. A single explanation for the regulator does not give the applicant a reason or help the team debug.","Incorrect. Asking the model afterwards produces a plausible story and not a reconstruction of what happened, so it cannot be relied on.","Incorrect. Explainability is built into the system through decision logging and design, not settled by choosing a model."],"df":"hard","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P246","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team plans to put an internal API key and a database password in its agent's system prompt, so that the agent can use them. The developer argues that users never see the system prompt. The security architect disagrees. Which TWO changes should be made? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"None, since users cannot see the system prompt and the model only uses the secrets through tools."},{"l":"B","t":"Keep the key and the password in the application's secret store, never in the prompt."},{"l":"C","t":"Shorten the system prompt, since the secrets make it too long and add latency."},{"l":"D","t":"Have the tools use the credentials on the server, so that the model never sees them."},{"l":"E","t":"Move the secrets out of the prompt only because they stop prompt caching from working."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Assume the system prompt can be extracted. Secrets stay in the application, and tools use them on the server without the model seeing them.","x":["Incorrect. System prompts can be extracted through injection.","Correct. A secret store keeps credentials out of anything the model can repeat.","Incorrect. Length is not the risk; exposure is.","Correct. Server-side use means the credentials never enter the model's context.","Incorrect. Caching is not the reason; extraction is."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P250","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Any employee can edit the knowledge base that a retailer's support agent retrieves from. Someone added a false refund policy, and the agent repeated it to customers for a week before anyone noticed. Which TWO controls address the cause? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Require approval before edits to the knowledge base go live."},{"l":"B","t":"A larger model, on the basis that it will notice which of the retrieved refund policies is false."},{"l":"C","t":"Track provenance and change history for every article."},{"l":"D","t":"An output filter that blocks any reply containing the word 'refund'."},{"l":"E","t":"A disclaimer telling customers that the policies the agent quotes may be inaccurate."}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Retrieved content is only as trustworthy as its governance. Approval, provenance and change history stop false content entering the corpus and show quickly where it came from.","x":["Correct. Approval stops false content going live.","Incorrect. A model cannot tell which official-looking policy is false.","Correct. Provenance and history show who changed what, and allow a quick rollback.","Incorrect. Blocking a word breaks legitimate refund answers.","Incorrect. A disclaimer still spreads the false policy."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P251","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A telecom built an agent to summarise support tickets. It was also given tools to close tickets, issue credits and email customers, 'in case they are useful', and last week it closed a ticket that was still open. Which TWO changes fix the underlying risk? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ground the summaries with retrieval from the ticketing system, so that they are more accurate."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the tools the summarising task does not need."},{"l":"C","t":"Limit the length of each summary, so that the agent has fewer tokens in which to take unexpected actions."},{"l":"D","t":"Pin the agent to a single cloud region, so that its actions stay within one jurisdiction."},{"l":"E","t":"Gate any consequential action that remains behind authorization or approval."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Tools beyond the task's needs create excessive agency. Remove them, and gate the consequential actions that remain.","x":["Incorrect. Accuracy is not the problem; the agent's power is.","Correct. A summariser has no need to close, credit or email.","Incorrect. Shorter summaries do not remove the tools.","Incorrect. Location has nothing to do with excessive agency.","Correct. A gate stops any remaining action from running unchecked."],"df":"hard","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P252","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A compliance assistant answers questions about regulations, some of which changed after the model's training data was collected. What is the right design response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell users that the model knows all current regulations."},{"l":"B","t":"Ground answers in current, retrieved regulatory text and show its effective date."},{"l":"C","t":"Wait for a newer model and make no design changes in the meantime."},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the temperature so that the model is less attached to its older knowledge."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Training data has a cutoff. Ground answers in current retrieved text and show effective dates so that users can judge currency.","x":["Incorrect. Telling users the model knows all current regulations is false, since its training data has a cutoff.","Correct. Ground answers in current retrieved regulatory text and show its effective date, so users can judge how current it is.","Incorrect. Waiting for a newer model leaves the same cutoff problem in place, and users get outdated answers meanwhile.","Incorrect. Temperature changes randomness and does not supply newer regulations."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P255","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team sets a confidence threshold for sending extractions to human review. How should the threshold be chosen?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pick 0.9, because it is a round number that sounds safe."},{"l":"B","t":"Use the model's own confidence scores exactly as they are given, without checking them, since the model knows best how sure it is."},{"l":"C","t":"Use calibration data relating confidence to actual error rates, and set the threshold for acceptable risk and review capacity."},{"l":"D","t":"Set the threshold so that exactly half of the items go to review."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Thresholds should come from calibration data linking confidence to actual errors, balanced against acceptable risk and reviewer capacity.","x":["Incorrect. A round number is arbitrary and not tied to how errors actually behave.","Incorrect. Model confidence scores are not reliably calibrated, so they need to be checked against actual outcomes.","Correct. Calibration data linking confidence to actual error rates, balanced against acceptable risk and review capacity, gives a defensible threshold.","Incorrect. A fixed fifty per cent ignores the real error distribution and may overwhelm reviewers or miss errors."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P257","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A European customer of an online retailer exercises their right to have their personal data erased. Their data appears in application logs, the retrieval index and cached conversation summaries, as well as the CRM. Which TWO things are required? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Delete the customer's record in the CRM only, since all the other stores are derived from it."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the model to forget the customer, so that it no longer uses their details."},{"l":"C","t":"Map every store that holds personal data at design time, so that erasure requests can be met."},{"l":"D","t":"Nothing, since data used by AI systems is exempt from erasure requests."},{"l":"E","t":"A deletion process that finds and removes the data in every store, including logs, indexes and caches."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Erasure must reach every copy of the data, including logs, indexes and caches. Map these stores at design time so that requests can be met.","x":["Incorrect. Logs, the index and the caches are separate copies.","Incorrect. A model cannot be asked to forget stored data.","Correct. A map of every store makes complete erasure possible.","Incorrect. AI systems are not exempt.","Correct. Every copy must be removed to meet the request."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P259","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A clinical assistant drafts appointment reminders. The team plans to include each patient's full medical history in every prompt 'for context'. Which principle applies?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Full history should always be included, since more context improves quality."},{"l":"B","t":"Data residency, which is the only HIPAA requirement relevant to prompts."},{"l":"C","t":"Minimum necessary: use only the PHI the task requires."},{"l":"D","t":"None, as long as a BAA is in place."}],"a":["C"],"r":"HIPAA's minimum necessary standard limits PHI to what the task requires. A BAA does not remove that obligation.","x":["Incorrect. More context is not always better, and sending full medical history for a reminder exposes patient data without need.","Incorrect. Data residency is not the only HIPAA requirement relevant to prompts, and it does not address how much PHI is sent.","Correct. The minimum necessary principle limits PHI to what the task requires, so an appointment reminder needs far less than a full history.","Incorrect. A BAA does not remove the minimum necessary obligation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P261","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team assumes that the provider retains no request data, because it saw this on a forum. Which action is correct before the design is approved?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Confirm the provider's retention terms for the specific route, contract, and features in use, and record the evidence."},{"l":"B","t":"Proceed on the assumption, since forum posts usually reflect current policy."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask Claude what the provider's data-retention policy is, and record its answer in the design document as the supporting evidence."},{"l":"D","t":"Assume all providers retain data forever and drop the project."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Retention terms depend on route, contract, and feature. Confirm them from authoritative sources and keep the evidence. A model's answer is not evidence.","x":["Correct. Retention depends on route, contract and feature, so confirm the terms for those in use and record the evidence.","Incorrect. Forum posts are not authoritative and may be outdated or wrong.","Incorrect. A model's answer about the provider's policy is not evidence and may be wrong.","Incorrect. Assuming all providers retain data forever is another guess, and dropping the project without checking is unjustified."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P264","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to test whether its CV-screening assistant treats candidates differently by gender. Which test fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the model whether it is biased and record its answer."},{"l":"B","t":"Check that the system prompt contains a line forbidding bias."},{"l":"C","t":"Counterfactual tests: submit matched CVs that differ only in gender-linked details and compare the outcomes."},{"l":"D","t":"Review a handful of decisions by hand each month and look for anything that seems unfair to the reviewer at the time."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Counterfactual tests isolate the attribute by changing only it. Self-reports and prompt lines are not measurements.","x":["Incorrect. A model's own statement that it is not biased is not a measurement.","Incorrect. A prompt line forbidding bias does not test whether outcomes differ.","Correct. Counterfactual tests change only the gender-linked details of otherwise matched CVs and compare outcomes, which isolates the effect.","Incorrect. A handful of manual reviews depends on what the reviewer happens to notice, and does not isolate the attribute."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P267","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Teams across a company want to use an internal Claude assistant for new purposes. What documentation helps them use it safely?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A list of the prompts used, with no further explanation."},{"l":"B","t":"A system card describing intended uses, known limitations, eval results, and uses that are not allowed."},{"l":"C","t":"The provider's marketing materials about Claude's capabilities."},{"l":"D","t":"No documentation is needed, since each team can test the assistant themselves and learn its limits by using it for a while."}],"a":["B"],"r":"A system card covering intended uses, limitations, eval results, and prohibited uses helps new teams adopt the assistant safely.","x":["Incorrect. A list of prompts without explanation does not tell teams what the assistant is for or where it fails.","Correct. A system card covering intended uses, known limitations, eval results and prohibited uses lets new teams adopt the assistant safely.","Incorrect. Marketing materials about capabilities do not describe this deployment's limits or rules.","Incorrect. Teams learning limits by trial exposes customers to the failures and is not documentation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P268","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"An assistant is found to have given dangerous advice to several customers. Which THREE steps belong in the response? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Contain the problem, for example by disabling the affected feature with a flag."},{"l":"B","t":"Delete the logs of the affected conversations to limit exposure."},{"l":"C","t":"Preserve the evidence and find the root cause."},{"l":"D","t":"Quietly change the prompt and tell no one."},{"l":"E","t":"Add the failing cases to the eval so that the fix is verified and the problem cannot return."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"Contain, preserve evidence and find the root cause, then lock in the fix with eval cases. Deleting logs or silent changes destroy accountability.","x":["Correct. Containment, for example disabling the affected feature with a flag, stops further harm.","Incorrect. Deleting logs destroys evidence and accountability, and does not reduce the harm.","Correct. Preserve the evidence and find the root cause so the fix addresses the real problem.","Incorrect. Quietly changing the prompt without telling anyone removes accountability and hides the incident.","Correct. Adding the failing cases to the eval verifies the fix and keeps the problem from coming back."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement guardrails and safety controls"},
{"id":"P88","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor says, 'We want this to feel seamless.' Which translation is best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Promise zero downtime in the statement of work so that the sponsor's expectation is met in writing."},{"l":"B","t":"Set it aside as a subjective preference that cannot be turned into a requirement or a design constraint."},{"l":"C","t":"Select the largest available model tier, since 'seamless' means the highest possible quality of response."},{"l":"D","t":"Treat it as a latency and graceful-failure requirement: set a p95 target and confirm the assumption."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Discovery listens, translates, and writes down. 'Seamless' implies a latency budget and failures that do not break the flow, and the assumption is recorded and confirmed.","x":["Incorrect. A zero-downtime promise written into the statement of work commits the team to something no system can guarantee. The sponsor's wish should be translated into a measurable target.","Incorrect. A subjective phrase can be turned into requirements. 'Seamless' points to latency and failure behaviour that can be specified.","Incorrect. Quality is not what 'seamless' means, and the largest tier adds latency and cost, which works against a seamless feel.","Correct. 'Seamless' implies a latency budget and failures that do not break the flow. Set a p95 target, record the assumption and confirm it with the sponsor."],"df":"easy","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P90","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor says, 'Clinicians will review the output anyway.' How should the architect translate this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reduce evaluation effort, because clinicians will catch any errors before they reach the patient record."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove detailed logging, because a named person is accountable for each note that is authorised."},{"l":"C","t":"Assume the review is optional and can be skipped when clinicians are under time pressure."},{"l":"D","t":"A mandatory human authorization gate before output enters the record; confirm authority and timing."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Where output has legal, financial, or clinical consequence, review is an architectural gate, not an informal safety net. Record the assumption and confirm authority and timing.","x":["Incorrect. Expecting clinicians to catch errors is not a reason to evaluate less. The review step has to be designed and tested.","Incorrect. A named person being accountable is a reason to keep detailed logs, since the record must show who authorised what.","Incorrect. Treating review as optional leaves nothing between a wrong output and the patient record when clinicians are busy.","Correct. Where output has clinical consequence, review is an architectural gate. Make authorisation mandatory before output enters the record and confirm who has the authority and when."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P93","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"yn","q":"A lender is agreeing a quality SLA with the business for its underwriting assistant before launch. The sponsor proposes 99.9% \"because it sounds professional\", and the pilot's best week scored 97%. For each statement about setting the threshold, select Yes if it is sound or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["The threshold should come from evaluation results and acceptance criteria agreed before launch.","The best score from the pilot week is a defensible threshold.","The SLA should say what is measured, what counts as a breach and what happens next.","A competitor's advertised figure is a sound basis for the threshold.","99.9% is a good choice, because it signals ambition to the sponsor.","Production quality should be measured on sampled traffic, the same way as in the evaluation."],"a":["Yes","No","Yes","No","No","Yes"],"r":"An SLA names what is measured, what counts as a breach and what happens next. Thresholds trace to something tangible; for quality, that is evaluation results and agreed acceptance criteria, measured the same way in production.","x":["Yes. Evidence and agreement make the threshold defensible.","No. A best week is not what the system delivers normally.","Yes. Without these, nobody knows when the SLA is broken.","No. A competitor's marketing says nothing about this system.","No. A number with no evidence behind it will be breached.","Yes. Measuring the same way keeps production comparable with the evaluation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P94","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's assistant saw its evaluation score drift downward for four weeks. The error rate stayed flat, so no alert fired, and the problem was found only at the quarterly review. The sponsor asks what must change. Which TWO additions are needed? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A decision layer that maps each signal, including slow drifts, to a trigger, an owner and an action."},{"l":"B","t":"A larger model tier, so that answer quality stops drifting downward over time."},{"l":"C","t":"A rule that an engineer reviews every request by hand before it reaches users."},{"l":"D","t":"An alert on the evaluation score's trend over time, not only on the error rate."},{"l":"E","t":"More dashboards that show the same metrics in additional views for stakeholders."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Monitoring is not a feedback loop. Observability supplies signals; the loop decides which matter through signals, triage, decision, action and review, with triggers and owners defined before launch, including for slow drifts.","x":["Correct. A trigger and an owner turn a drift into an action.","Incorrect. A larger tier does not make anyone notice the next drift.","Incorrect. Reviewing every request is unworkable.","Correct. A trend alert catches a slow decline the error rate misses.","Incorrect. More views of the same metrics still trigger nothing."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P95","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"match","q":"The operations team for a bank's assistant collects signals every week, and the architect runs the triage step of the feedback loop. Match each signal to where it should go. A destination can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Noise: no action"},{"l":"B","t":"Internal monitoring"},{"l":"C","t":"Architect review"},{"l":"D","t":"Stakeholder review"}],"st":["A one-minute spike of rate-limit errors, fully absorbed by retries with backoff.","Daily token use that stays inside the forecast band.","A diagnosed, sustained quality decline that threatens the agreed quality SLA.","One user's typo in a question, which the assistant handled without difficulty.","Retrieval precision drifting down for two weeks, with the cause not yet known.","A forecast showing that spend will pass the agreed budget next quarter."],"a":["A","B","D","A","C","D"],"r":"Triage sorts signals into internal monitoring, architect review, stakeholder review and noise. Absorbed errors and in-forecast use need nothing more; an undiagnosed drift needs the architect; a confirmed threat to an agreement belongs with the stakeholder.","x":["Noise. The retries absorbed it, and nobody was affected.","Internal monitoring. It is on plan, so keep watching.","Stakeholder review. A threat to the agreed SLA is the stakeholder's decision.","Noise. Nothing went wrong.","Architect review. A drift with no known cause needs diagnosis.","Stakeholder review. A budget breach needs the sponsor's decision."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P96","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A hospital's assistant is subject to data-residency rules, and its monitoring relied only on alerts. Months later an auditor asked for proof that residency had been confirmed regularly, and the team had none. Which TWO things were missing? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A statement from the platform vendor's marketing material saying that the service complies with residency rules."},{"l":"B","t":"Scheduled review checkpoints, set up at design time with triggers and owners, that run even when nothing is wrong."},{"l":"C","t":"A larger model for the compliance checks, so that residency problems are noticed more reliably."},{"l":"D","t":"A higher alert threshold, so that fewer alerts are raised and each one carries more weight."},{"l":"E","t":"The evidence from each check, kept so that it can be shown to an auditor later."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Some regulated reviews must run on a schedule even when nothing has gone wrong, and their evidence must be kept. If a checkpoint is never wired to a trigger, a violation can run for weeks before routine review catches it.","x":["Incorrect. A vendor's marketing is not evidence for this deployment.","Correct. Scheduled checks confirm residency whether or not an alert fires.","Incorrect. A larger model does not create the missing record.","Incorrect. Fewer alerts make it less likely that problems are seen.","Correct. Kept evidence is what the auditor asked for."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P102","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Six months after launch an assistant's quality is acceptable, but cost per request has tripled as conversations grew longer, and the monolithic context strategy is the cause. Which judgement fits the 'decide' step of the feedback loop?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Re-architect rather than iterate:** the context strategy is the cause, so prompt or threshold changes cannot reach it."},{"l":"B","t":"**Iterate:** tune the prompt wording and the `max_tokens` cap, then re-measure the cost at the end of next quarter."},{"l":"C","t":"**Iterate:** move to a cheaper model tier, which lowers cost without changing anything else about the design."},{"l":"D","t":"**Neither:** rising cost at higher volume is expected, so it should be reported to the sponsor and accepted."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The loop runs signals, triage, decide, act, review, and the decide step separates what the team can iterate on from a cause that sits in the architecture. When the cost driver is the context strategy itself, tuning around it does not reach the cause and the design decision has to be reopened with the stakeholder.","x":["Correct. The context strategy is the cause, so changes to prompts or thresholds cannot reach it. The design decision needs to be reopened.","Incorrect. Tuning wording and a token cap does not change a monolithic context strategy, and waiting a quarter to re-measure delays the real decision.","Incorrect. A cheaper tier lowers the unit price but leaves the growing context unchanged, and it may reduce quality that is currently acceptable.","Incorrect. A tripled cost per request caused by the architecture is not simply expected. It needs a decision rather than acceptance."],"df":"hard","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P273","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A discovery workshop for a claims assistant includes only the business sponsor and the development team. Which gap is most likely to cause problems later?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Too few developers, which will slow the build."},{"l":"B","t":"Missing compliance, operations, and end-user voices, whose requirements will surface late."},{"l":"C","t":"Too many questions about current processes, which wastes the sponsor's time."},{"l":"D","t":"No vendor representative in the room to confirm what the model can and cannot do for this particular use case."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Compliance, operations, and end users each hold requirements the sponsor may not mention. Missing them in discovery means late surprises.","x":["Incorrect. The number of developers is not the main risk in discovery.","Correct. Compliance, operations and end users each hold requirements the sponsor may not mention, and missing them means late surprises.","Incorrect. Asking about current processes is valuable discovery and not a waste of time.","Incorrect. A vendor representative is not what is missing. The missing perspectives are those of the people who use and govern the process."],"df":"easy","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P275","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"yn","q":"In a discovery workshop for a claims assistant, the sponsor says, 'The assistant must be 100% accurate.' The architect needs to turn this into a requirement the team can build and test against. For each statement, select Yes if it is a sound response or No if it is not.","o":[],"st":["The requirement should be turned into acceptable error rates for each type of error.","Controls that catch errors, such as review for high-risk cases, belong in the requirement.","Different errors can carry different tolerances, such as a wrong payout versus a typo.","The sentence can go into the statement of work as written.","The architect should refuse the project, since no AI system is 100% accurate.","Using the largest model tier will meet the 100% target."],"a":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],"r":"Turn absolute language into measurable requirements: tolerable error rates by type, and the controls that catch errors. Refusing or accepting it as written both fail the sponsor.","x":["Yes. Error rates can be measured and tested.","Yes. Controls make the remaining errors safe.","Yes. Errors differ in cost, so their tolerances differ.","No. An untestable sentence cannot be built or accepted against.","No. The sponsor's need is real; it just needs translating.","No. No model tier is 100% accurate."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P278","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"At an insurer, legal wants every AI reply reviewed by a person, while operations wants full automation. Both report to the same sponsor, and the project has stalled for a month. Which TWO things should the architect do? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Side with operations, since automation is the purpose of the project."},{"l":"B","t":"Side with legal, since compliance requirements always outrank efficiency goals."},{"l":"C","t":"Have the sponsor, as decision owner, decide, and record the decision."},{"l":"D","t":"Build both versions and let each team use whichever version it prefers."},{"l":"E","t":"Propose risk-tiered review, with the cost and risk of each option."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Conflicting requirements are decisions for the accountable owner. Surface the trade-off, offer options with their costs and risks, and get a decision recorded.","x":["Incorrect. The architect is not the decision owner.","Incorrect. Neither side automatically outranks the other; the owner decides.","Correct. A recorded decision by the owner ends the stall.","Incorrect. Two versions double the cost and avoid the decision.","Correct. Risk tiers give the sponsor a middle option with known costs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Communicate architectural decisions and trade-offs"},
{"id":"P280","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"An executive asks, 'Will the AI ever get it wrong?' Which answer best supports a sound decision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"'No, the model is very accurate and has been tested.'"},{"l":"B","t":"'Yes, AI systems sometimes hallucinate, so there will always be some risk of a wrong answer in production whatever we do about it.'"},{"l":"C","t":"'In testing it is wrong on about 3 in 100 requests; this control catches most of those, and here is the cost of the rest.'"},{"l":"D","t":"'It depends on many technical factors that are hard to explain.'"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Executives need probabilistic behaviour expressed in business terms: rates, controls, and residual cost. Vague or absolute answers do not support a decision.","x":["Incorrect. 'No' is an absolute that is not true and sets up a false expectation.","Incorrect. This is vague and gives the executive no figures or controls to decide on.","Correct. It states the error rate in business terms, what catches most errors, and the cost of the remainder, which supports a decision.","Incorrect. An answer that avoids the question leaves the executive unable to decide."],"df":"medium","ob":"Communicate architectural decisions and trade-offs"},
{"id":"P282","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A sponsor wants a full multi-agent assistant for loan servicing live in four weeks, ahead of a board meeting. The architect's estimate for that scope is twelve weeks, including evaluation and testing. Which TWO responses are best? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"State plainly the risks of compressing the full scope into four weeks."},{"l":"B","t":"Agree to four weeks, and ask the team to work faster to hit the date."},{"l":"C","t":"Refuse to start until the sponsor accepts the twelve-week estimate in writing."},{"l":"D","t":"Offer a phased plan: what can safely ship in four weeks, and what follows."},{"l":"E","t":"Agree to four weeks, but skip evaluation and testing so that the full scope fits the deadline."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Offer a phased path that fits the deadline safely, and state the risks of compressing it. Cutting evaluation hides risk rather than removing it.","x":["Correct. The sponsor needs the risks to make an informed choice.","Incorrect. Working faster does not turn twelve weeks into four.","Incorrect. Refusing ends the conversation instead of solving it.","Correct. A phase that ships safely meets the board date.","Incorrect. Skipping testing hides the risk until production."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P284","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A sponsor wants a quality SLA for an assistant. Which TWO elements make it enforceable? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A statement that quality will be 'excellent'."},{"l":"B","t":"A target chosen to impress the sponsor's leadership."},{"l":"C","t":"A defined measurement method, sample, and window."},{"l":"D","t":"An exclusion of every case where the model is wrong."},{"l":"E","t":"An agreed response when the threshold is breached."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"An SLA names what is measured, how, and what happens on breach. Adjectives and exclusions of every failure make it unenforceable.","x":["Incorrect. A statement that quality will be 'excellent' cannot be measured or enforced.","Incorrect. A target chosen to impress rather than from evidence is not defensible or enforceable.","Correct. A defined measurement method, sample and window make the SLA measurable.","Incorrect. Excluding every case where the model is wrong makes the SLA meaningless.","Correct. An agreed response when the threshold is breached makes the SLA enforceable."],"df":"hard","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P288","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"The model version a production system uses is scheduled for retirement in four months. What is the best lifecycle response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Wait until the retirement date, then switch to whichever model the provider recommends as the replacement at that time."},{"l":"B","t":"Switch to the newest model today without testing, to finish early."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the provider to keep the old model running for this customer."},{"l":"D","t":"Plan the migration: evaluate candidate models on the golden dataset, schedule the change, and tell stakeholders."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Model retirement is a planned lifecycle event. Evaluate candidates, schedule the change, and communicate, rather than switching blind.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting until retirement and switching to a recommended model leaves no time to evaluate it or tell stakeholders.","Incorrect. Switching to the newest model untested risks quality regressions with no evidence.","Incorrect. Retirement dates are set by the provider and a model cannot be kept running for one customer, so this is not a plan.","Correct. Treat retirement as a planned lifecycle event. Evaluate candidates on the golden dataset, schedule the change and tell stakeholders."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P292","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Which THREE items belong in a production-readiness checklist for a Claude application? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Evals pass the agreed thresholds on the current golden dataset."},{"l":"B","t":"Monitoring and alerts are in place, each with a named owner."},{"l":"C","t":"The sponsor has approved a demo."},{"l":"D","t":"A tested rollback plan exists."},{"l":"E","t":"The largest model tier is selected."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"Readiness means evidence of quality, owned monitoring, and a tested way back. A demo and a large model are not readiness.","x":["Correct. Evals passing the agreed thresholds on the current golden dataset is evidence of quality.","Correct. Monitoring and alerts with named owners mean problems are noticed and someone is responsible.","Incorrect. A sponsor's approval of a demo is not evidence that the application is ready for production.","Correct. A tested rollback plan gives a way back if the release causes problems.","Incorrect. The largest tier is not a readiness criterion. The model should be chosen by evals, cost and latency."],"df":"hard","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P293","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A pilot assistant is being retired. What must the retirement plan include?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Turning off the user interface; nothing else is needed."},{"l":"B","t":"Keep all of the pilot's data indefinitely, in case it is needed later for a future project or an audit."},{"l":"C","t":"Asking the model to stop responding."},{"l":"D","t":"Handling stored data under retention rules, notifying users, and revoking keys and integrations."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Retirement covers data under retention rules, user communication, and revoking credentials and integrations, so that nothing lingers.","x":["Incorrect. Turning off the interface leaves data, keys and integrations in place.","Incorrect. Keeping all pilot data indefinitely conflicts with retention rules and increases risk.","Incorrect. Asking the model to stop responding does not retire the system or its data and credentials.","Correct. Retirement covers stored data under retention rules, notifying users, and revoking keys and integrations."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P298","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Which risk-register entry is most useful?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"'Retrieval quality may drop as the corpus grows; likelihood medium; impact high; mitigation: weekly recall@k check; owner named; trigger: recall below 0.85.'"},{"l":"B","t":"'AI risk: high. The team will monitor the model closely and act quickly if anything goes wrong after launch.'"},{"l":"C","t":"'The model might hallucinate sometimes, which could affect customers; the team should keep this in mind during the project.'"},{"l":"D","t":"'Risks to be discussed at the next steering meeting, once the system is in production and real data is available.'"}],"a":["A"],"r":"A useful risk entry names likelihood, impact, mitigation, owner, and a measurable trigger. Vague entries cannot be acted on.","x":["Correct. It names the risk, likelihood, impact, mitigation, an owner and a measurable trigger, so someone can act on it.","Incorrect. 'AI risk: high' with a promise to monitor is vague and has no owner, measure or trigger.","Incorrect. 'Keep this in mind' gives no mitigation, owner or trigger.","Incorrect. Deferring the discussion until production means the risk is not managed during the build."],"df":"hard","ob":"Document architectures and provide implementation guidance"},
{"id":"P299","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Mid-project, the sponsor asks to add Hindi and Tamil support. What is the best way to handle the request?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree immediately and add it to the current sprint."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the request, since the scope was agreed and fixed at the start of the project and cannot change now."},{"l":"C","t":"Assess the impact on scope, evals, cost, and timeline, and agree the change through change control."},{"l":"D","t":"Add the languages quietly without updating the plan."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Change control assesses impact on scope, evals, cost, and timeline before agreeing. Language support needs new eval slices, not just a configuration change.","x":["Incorrect. Agreeing at once and adding it to the sprint ignores the cost, the timeline and the evals needed.","Incorrect. Refusing outright is inflexible. A changed requirement should be assessed and not simply rejected.","Correct. Assess the impact on scope, evals, cost and timeline and agree the change through change control.","Incorrect. Adding languages quietly without updating the plan leaves them untested and untracked."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P103","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"An organisation wants a code-review procedure used identically by all departments, centrally revocable, with version-controlled updates and rollback. Which distribution mechanism is the strongest fit?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A Claude Code project Skill kept in the repository of a single team and shared by copy."},{"l":"B","t":"Each developer installs the Skill manually from a shared link and updates it when they remember."},{"l":"C","t":"An org-provisioned Skill, because it offers native version pinning and an automatic rollback path."},{"l":"D","t":"A plugin assigned to a group or org, with version-controlled updates from a connected repository."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Plugins give group and org targeting, install preferences, and version-controlled updates with a path back. Org-provisioned Skills reach everyone but have no native version pinning or rollback, and project Skills are scoped to the repositories that carry them.","x":["Incorrect. A Skill in one team's repository shared by copy is scoped to that repository and spreads as unmanaged copies.","Incorrect. Manual installs from a shared link leave every developer on a different version, with no central control.","Incorrect. Org-provisioned Skills reach everyone but have no native version pinning or rollback path.","Correct. A plugin assigned to a group or org gives central targeting and revocation, version-controlled updates from a connected repository, and a path back."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P107","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO failure modes should a team rollout of AI tooling be designed to prevent? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Appointing one champion in each department to prove the workflow before wider rollout."},{"l":"B","t":"Stalling at basic chat, where the team never advances to repository-aware assistance, tool use, or packaged Skills."},{"l":"C","t":"Lumpy adoption, where a few developers use the tools heavily and the rest barely touch them."},{"l":"D","t":"Starting every team member from the same shared baseline configuration and permission posture."},{"l":"E","t":"Setting model allowlists, rate limits, and per-user spend caps as part of the team setup."}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Champion-and-batch rollout spreads usage deliberately, and enablement beyond first-step access prevents stalling. A shared configuration and spend posture are part of good setup, not failure modes.","x":["Incorrect. One champion per department proving the workflow first is a good rollout practice, not a failure mode.","Correct. Stalling at basic chat means the team never reaches repository-aware assistance, tool use or Skills, so enablement must go beyond first-step access.","Correct. Lumpy adoption, with a few heavy users and many who barely use the tools, is a failure mode that a deliberate rollout should prevent.","Incorrect. A shared baseline configuration and permission posture is part of good setup, not a failure mode.","Incorrect. Allowlists, rate limits and per-user spend caps are sound controls, not something to prevent."],"df":"medium","ob":"Improve developer workflows using AI-assisted tooling"},
{"id":"P108","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"AI-generated code passes all tests, but the submitting developer cannot explain how it handles inputs outside the test set. Which verification-checklist dimension has failed?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Security only, because a developer who cannot explain the code has probably left a vulnerability in it."},{"l":"B","t":"Maintainability only, because the formatting differs slightly from the team's usual style."},{"l":"C","t":"Human understanding: the author must be able to explain the code, including untested inputs."},{"l":"D","t":"Correctness, because the tests failed and the change should therefore never have reached review."}],"a":["C"],"r":"The four dimensions are correctness, security, maintainability, and human understanding. Green tests are not understanding; this gate stops judgment erosion from reaching production.","x":["Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario shows a vulnerability. The failed dimension is that the author does not understand the code, which is not only a security matter.","Incorrect. Style differences are not the issue here. The problem is the author's inability to explain behaviour on untested inputs.","Correct. Human understanding has failed. Passing tests does not mean the author can explain how the code handles inputs outside the test set.","Incorrect. The tests passed, so correctness on the tested cases is not what failed. The gap is understanding."],"df":"medium","ob":"Improve developer workflows using AI-assisted tooling"},
{"id":"P109","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Latency has spiked in a production Claude system with no code change. Which TWO first actions are most appropriate? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rotate the API keys, because stale credentials can slow down every request."},{"l":"B","t":"Confirm prompt-cache behaviour, since a cache that stopped hitting raises latency and cost."},{"l":"C","t":"Move all traffic to a larger model tier, which usually speeds responses up on its own."},{"l":"D","t":"Rewrite the system prompt's tone and phrasing, since wording changes drive latency."},{"l":"E","t":"Use traces to find the slowest span, checking tokens per request and the slowest tool call."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Latency spikes usually trace to context growth, a slow tool, or a cache that stopped hitting. The first action is to find the slowest span with telemetry and confirm cache behaviour before changing anything.","x":["Incorrect. Stale credentials cause authentication errors, not slow responses, so rotating keys is not a first step for latency.","Correct. A prompt cache that stopped hitting raises both latency and cost, so confirm cache behaviour early.","Incorrect. Larger tiers are usually slower, and moving all traffic is a big change before finding the cause.","Incorrect. Tone and phrasing changes do not drive latency, and changing the prompt before diagnosing is guesswork.","Correct. Traces show the slowest span, with tokens per request and the slowest tool call, so you fix the actual cause."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support debugging and operational issue resolution"},
{"id":"P110","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"match","q":"A platform team is configuring Claude Code for a 50-person engineering group. It wants each need met by the feature built for it, so that the main context stays lean and shared tools are built once. Match each need to the feature that fits it. A feature can be used more than once.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The project `CLAUDE.md`"},{"l":"B","t":"A Skill"},{"l":"C","t":"A subagent with its own context"},{"l":"D","t":"An MCP server"}],"st":["Repository conventions that every session should know.","A release-notes procedure that should run the same way every time.","Codebase exploration that should not clutter the main conversation.","A ticket tool that Claude Code, Claude.ai and an internal web app all need.","A long security review whose verbose output should stay out of the main thread.","A data-migration checklist with bundled scripts, loaded only when it is needed."],"a":["A","B","C","D","C","B"],"r":"`CLAUDE.md` carries persistent context for every task; Skills package repeatable procedures loaded on demand; subagents give bounded work its own context; MCP serves a tool to more than one client.","x":["CLAUDE.md. Conventions apply to every session.","Skill. A repeatable procedure, loaded when needed.","Subagent. Exploration runs in its own context.","MCP server. One server is reachable from all three clients.","Subagent. Verbose output stays in the subagent's context.","Skill. Skills bundle instructions and scripts and load on demand."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P304","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A repository contains a `.env` file with credentials. The team wants Claude Code never to read it. Which configuration is most direct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A deny rule for reading that file in the permission settings."},{"l":"B","t":"A line in `CLAUDE.md` asking Claude not to open the file."},{"l":"C","t":"Renaming the file so that its name does not suggest secrets."},{"l":"D","t":"Moving the file to the repository root so that it is more visible."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Permission deny rules are enforced. `CLAUDE.md` is context, not enforcement, and renaming or moving the file changes nothing.","x":["Correct. A permission deny rule for reading that file is enforced by Claude Code.","Incorrect. A line in CLAUDE.md is context, not enforcement, so Claude could still open the file.","Incorrect. Renaming the file does not stop Claude reading it.","Incorrect. Moving the file to the repository root makes it more visible and does not restrict access."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P305","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants every developer to have the same MCP servers when they work in a repository with Claude Code. Some servers need API tokens. Which TWO practices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each developer to add the servers by hand following a wiki page."},{"l":"B","t":"Commit a project-scoped MCP configuration file to the repository."},{"l":"C","t":"Reference tokens through environment variables rather than committing them."},{"l":"D","t":"Commit the API tokens in the configuration file so that setup is automatic."},{"l":"E","t":"Share one developer's user-level configuration by email."}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"A project-scoped MCP configuration committed to the repository gives everyone the same servers, and environment variables keep secrets out of version control.","x":["Incorrect. Manual setup from a wiki page leads to inconsistent configurations between developers.","Correct. A project-scoped MCP configuration file committed to the repository gives every developer the same servers.","Correct. Referencing tokens through environment variables keeps secrets out of version control while the configuration stays shared.","Incorrect. Committing tokens exposes secrets to everyone with repository access and in its history.","Incorrect. Emailing one developer's user-level configuration spreads personal settings and possibly secrets, and is not a shared repository setup."],"df":"hard","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P312","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A production agent intermittently reports 'tool call failed' on a supplier lookup. What is the best first step?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use traces to find failing calls, inspect their inputs and the tool's logs, and reproduce with the same inputs."},{"l":"B","t":"Switch to a larger model, which will call the tool correctly."},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the supplier tool until the problem goes away."},{"l":"D","t":"Add a line to the system prompt asking the agent to retry the supplier lookup until it works, however many attempts that takes."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Debug from evidence: find the failing spans, inspect inputs and tool logs, and reproduce. Model changes and prompt nudges guess at the cause.","x":["Correct. Use traces to find the failing calls, inspect their inputs and the tool's logs, and reproduce with the same inputs.","Incorrect. Switching to a larger model guesses at the cause and does not fix a tool failure.","Incorrect. Removing the tool takes away the feature instead of finding why the call fails.","Incorrect. Asking the agent to retry endlessly can hide the failure and waste calls, without finding the cause."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support debugging and operational issue resolution"}
];
